$CATEGORY: $module$/t00_oop_fundamentals

::t00_Q1:: What is the relationship between a class and an object?
{
  = A class describes structure and behaviour; an object is one instance holding its own field values.
    #Correct. One class can produce any number of objects, each with independent state.
  ~ They are two words for the same thing.
    #Incorrect. The class is the template; the object is what exists at runtime.
  ~ An object describes the structure and a class is one instance of it.
    #Incorrect. This reverses the two terms.
  ~ A class holds the data and an object holds the methods.
    #Incorrect. A class declares both fields and methods; an object holds the field values.
}

::t00_Q2:: Why should instance fields normally be declared `private`?
{
  = So the class controls every change to its own state and its validation cannot be bypassed.
    #Correct. Public fields allow any code to write an invalid value directly, defeating guard clauses.
  ~ Because private fields use less memory.
    #Incorrect. Access modifiers have no effect on memory usage.
  ~ Because private fields are initialised automatically and public ones are not.
    #Incorrect. All instance fields receive default values regardless of access modifier.
  ~ Because Java requires all fields to be private.
    #Incorrect. Other modifiers are legal; private is a design convention, not a rule.
}

::t00_Q3:: [Predict the output] `Player p1 \= new Player("Ann"); Player p2 \= p1; p2.setName("Bo");`
What does `p1.getName()` return?
{
  = Bo
    #Correct. Both variables refer to the same object, so a change through one is visible through the other.
  ~ Ann
    #Incorrect. That would require p2 to be a separate copy, which assignment does not create.
  ~ null
    #Incorrect. The name was set at construction and then changed; it is never null.
  ~ The code throws NullPointerException.
    #Incorrect. Both references point at a valid object.
}

::t00_Q4:: Why should a guard clause appear at the top of a constructor rather than
where the field is later used?
{
  = So an object can never exist in an invalid state, and the error is reported at the point of the mistake.
    #Correct. Validating later means a bad object may be stored and passed around before anything complains.
  ~ Because guard clauses must be the first statement in Java.
    #Incorrect. There is no such language rule; this is a design convention.
  ~ Because constructors run faster than methods.
    #Incorrect. Performance is irrelevant to the reason.
  ~ Because fields cannot be validated after assignment.
    #Incorrect. They can; doing so is simply later than it should be.
}

::t00_Q5:: What is the purpose of `this(...)` in a constructor?
{
  = It delegates to another constructor in the same class, so validation lives in one place.
    #Correct. Copying guard clauses into several constructors invites them to drift apart.
  ~ It calls the parent class constructor.
    #Incorrect. That is `super(...)`.
  ~ It returns the current object to the caller.
    #Incorrect. Constructors have no return value; `this` alone is a reference to the current object.
  ~ It creates a second instance of the class.
    #Incorrect. Only one object is created; the delegated constructor initialises it.
}

::t00_Q6:: [Spot the bug] A constructor is written as `public Player(String name) \{ name \= name; \}`
where the field is also called `name`. Why does the field stay null?
{
  = The parameter shadows the field, so the assignment copies the parameter onto itself.
    #Correct. Write `this.name \= name;` to disambiguate, which is one reason many codebases prefix fields.
  ~ Constructors cannot assign to fields.
    #Incorrect. Assigning fields is their main purpose.
  ~ The field must be declared final for the assignment to take effect.
    #Incorrect. Final fields must be assigned in the constructor, but that is not the problem here.
  ~ String fields must be initialised with `new String(...)`.
    #Incorrect. Direct assignment of a String reference is normal.
}

::t00_Q7:: Which access modifier allows a member to be seen by subclasses and by
other classes in the same package, but not by unrelated outside classes?
{
  = protected
    #Correct. It is broader than private and narrower than public, and is intended for inheritance hooks.
  ~ private
    #Incorrect. Private members are visible only within the declaring class.
  ~ public
    #Incorrect. Public members are visible everywhere.
  ~ No modifier (package-private)
    #Incorrect. This gives package access but excludes subclasses in other packages.
}

::t00_Q8:: Which of the four OOP pillars does making fields private and exposing
methods most directly demonstrate?
{
  = Encapsulation
    #Correct. Internal state is hidden and access is mediated by the class's own methods.
  ~ Inheritance
    #Incorrect. Inheritance concerns deriving one class from another.
  ~ Polymorphism
    #Incorrect. Polymorphism concerns one reference type having several implementations.
  ~ Abstraction
    #Incorrect. Related, but abstraction is about exposing what something does rather than how it does it.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t01_arrays

::t01_Q1:: A Java method receives an `int[]` parameter and loops with `for (int i \= 0; i <\= arr.length; i++)`. What happens when the loop runs?
{
  = An ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException is thrown on the final iteration.
    #Correct. When i equals arr.length the index is one past the last valid position (0 to length-1), causing the exception.
  ~ The loop completes normally because the condition uses <=.
    #Incorrect. The condition `i <= arr.length` allows i to reach arr.length, which is an invalid index.
  ~ The last element is visited twice because of the <= boundary.
    #Incorrect. The loop will attempt to access arr[arr.length], which does not exist, and throw an exception.
  ~ A NullPointerException is thrown because the array has no element at that position.
    #Incorrect. A missing element at an out-of-bounds index causes ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException, not NullPointerException.
}

::t01_Q2:: You declare `int[][] jag \= new int[3][]`. After this single line,
which statement is true?
{
  = The three inner row references are null and must each be separately allocated before use.
    #Correct. `new int[3][]` allocates the outer array of three row references but leaves each inner row as null.
  ~ A 3x3 rectangular array of zeros is created automatically.
    #Incorrect. Only `new int[3][3]` would create a rectangular array; `new int[3][]` leaves rows unallocated.
  ~ Accessing `jag[0][0]` returns 0 because Java initialises inner arrays to zero by default.
    #Incorrect. Accessing `jag[0][0]` throws a NullPointerException because `jag[0]` is null.
  ~ The code does not compile because jagged arrays are not supported in Java.
    #Incorrect. Java fully supports jagged (non-rectangular) 2D arrays.
}

::t01_Q3:: A method needs to compute the average of an `int[]`. The following
line appears inside the method\: `double avg \= sum / xs.length;` where `sum`
is declared as `int`. For the input `\{3, 7\}` the result is `0.0` instead of `5.0`.
What is the most likely cause?
{
  = Integer division is performed before the result is widened to double.
    #Correct. Both `sum` and `xs.length` are ints, so division truncates before the double assignment.
  ~ The array length returns 0 for a two-element array.
    #Incorrect. `xs.length` returns 2 for a two-element array.
  ~ The enhanced for-loop calculates the sum incorrectly for integer arrays.
    #Incorrect. The issue is the division, not the loop. The sum itself would be correct.
  ~ Java rounds the average down to the nearest integer automatically.
    #Incorrect. Java performs integer division when both operands are int types, which truncates rather than rounds.
}

::t01_Q4:: You want to iterate over an `int[]` and double the value of every
element. Which loop achieves this correctly?
{
  = An index-based for loop using `xs[i] = xs[i] * 2` inside the body.
    #Correct. Only an index loop can write back to the original array; the loop variable gives the position needed to update.
  ~ An enhanced for loop with `val = val * 2` inside the body.
    #Incorrect. In an enhanced for loop, `val` is a copy of the element, so assigning to it does not modify the array.
  ~ Either loop type works because the array is passed by reference.
    #Incorrect. While the array reference is passed, the enhanced for loop variable is a primitive copy and cannot write back.
  ~ A while loop is required because for loops cannot modify array elements.
    #Incorrect. A standard index-based for loop can modify array elements; the loop type is not the constraint.
}


::t01_Q5:: [Predict the output] Given `int[] a \= \{1, 2, 3\};` followed by
`int[] b \= a;` then `b[0] \= 99;` what does `System.out.println(a[0])` print?
{
  = 99
    #Correct. Arrays are objects, so `b \= a` copies the reference, not the contents. Both names refer to one array.
  ~ 1
    #Incorrect. That would hold if `b` were a copy. Use `a.clone()` or `Arrays.copyOf(a, a.length)` to get a real copy.
  ~ 0
    #Incorrect. The element is set to 1 at creation and then to 99; it is never 0.
  ~ The code throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.
    #Incorrect. Index 0 is valid for a three-element array.
}

::t01_Q6:: [Spot the bug] This method should return the largest value, but for
the input `\{-5, -2, -9\}` it returns 0. The body is: `int best \= 0;` then a loop
`for (int i \= 0; i < xs.length; i++)` containing `if (xs[i] > best) best \= xs[i];`
then `return best;`. What is the fault?
{
  = `best` is seeded with 0 instead of the first element, so it is never replaced when every value is negative.
    #Correct. Seed with `int best \= xs[0];` and loop from index 1, after guarding against a null or empty array.
  ~ The loop should start at index 1 rather than index 0.
    #Incorrect. Starting at 1 is only correct once `best` is seeded from `xs[0]`; on its own it does not fix the wrong seed.
  ~ The comparison should be `>\=` rather than `>`.
    #Incorrect. Both find the maximum; only which of several equal values is kept changes.
  ~ The method should return `xs[best]` rather than `best`.
    #Incorrect. `best` already holds a value, not an index.
}

::t01_Q7:: You must visit every cell of a jagged 2D array `int[][] g` without
risking an exception. Which pair of loop conditions is correct?
{
  = Outer `r < g.length`, inner `c < g[r].length`
    #Correct. The column bound is read per row, so rows of differing lengths are all handled.
  ~ Outer `r < g.length`, inner `c < g[0].length`
    #Incorrect. This applies row 0's length to every row and throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException on any longer row.
  ~ Outer `r < g[0].length`, inner `c < g.length`
    #Incorrect. The bounds are swapped. `g.length` is the number of rows, not the number of columns.
  ~ Outer `r <\= g.length`, inner `c <\= g[r].length`
    #Incorrect. Both conditions run one index past the end of their array.
}

::t01_Q8:: What does `new int[3]` guarantee about its contents immediately after creation?
{
  = Every element holds 0, because Java initialises array elements to the type's default value.
    #Correct. `int` defaults to 0, `double` to 0.0, `boolean` to false, and reference types to null.
  ~ The contents are undefined until explicitly assigned.
    #Incorrect. That is the behaviour in C. Java always initialises array elements to type defaults.
  ~ Every element holds null.
    #Incorrect. `int` is a primitive and cannot be null. An `Integer[3]` would be filled with nulls.
  ~ The array has length 0 until values are added.
    #Incorrect. The length is fixed at 3 from creation and never changes.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t02_recursion

::t02_Q1:: What two parts must every correct recursive method have?
{
  = A base case that returns without recursing, and a recursive case that moves strictly closer to it.
    #Correct. Both are required. A base case that is never approached still produces infinite recursion.
  ~ A loop and a return statement.
    #Incorrect. Recursion replaces the loop; the method calls itself instead.
  ~ A static modifier and a return type.
    #Incorrect. These are ordinary method requirements, unrelated to recursion.
  ~ A try/catch for StackOverflowError.
    #Incorrect. Catching that error hides the bug rather than fixing the missing base case.
}

::t02_Q2:: [Predict the output] For `factorial(4)` implemented as
`if (n <\= 1) return 1; else return n * factorial(n - 1);` at what point does
the first multiplication actually occur?
{
  = After the base case returns, as the stack unwinds.
    #Correct. Frames pile up on the way down; the multiplications happen on the way back up, producing 1, 2, 6, 24.
  ~ Immediately, on the first call.
    #Incorrect. The first call cannot multiply until the value of factorial(3) is known.
  ~ Before any recursive call is made.
    #Incorrect. The recursive call appears as an operand of the multiplication and is evaluated first.
  ~ Never; the method returns 1 for all inputs.
    #Incorrect. That would only happen if the recursive branch were unreachable.
}

::t02_Q3:: Why is naive recursive Fibonacci unacceptably slow for n around 40?
{
  = The same subproblems are recomputed enormously many times, giving roughly O(2^n) work.
    #Correct. fib(n) branches into fib(n-1) and fib(n-2), whose call trees overlap almost entirely.
  ~ Recursion is always slower than iteration in Java.
    #Incorrect. The overhead per call is small; the problem here is repeated work.
  ~ It exceeds the stack depth limit at n \= 40.
    #Incorrect. The depth is only about 40 frames. Time, not depth, is the issue.
  ~ int overflows before n reaches 40.
    #Incorrect. fib(40) fits in an int; the method is slow well before any overflow concern.
}

::t02_Q4:: What technique fixes naive Fibonacci, and what does it cost?
{
  = Memoisation - caching computed results - which reduces time to O(n) at the cost of O(n) memory.
    #Correct. A classic time-for-space trade. An iterative version achieves the same time with O(1) memory.
  ~ Increasing the JVM stack size.
    #Incorrect. The problem is repeated computation, not stack depth.
  ~ Converting the parameters from int to long.
    #Incorrect. This changes the range of representable results, not the number of calls.
  ~ Making the method static.
    #Incorrect. This has no effect on the algorithm's complexity.
}

::t02_Q5:: [Spot the bug] A method throws StackOverflowError even though it has a
base case `if (n \=\= 0) return 0;`. It recurses with `return n + sum(n);`. What is wrong?
{
  = The argument never decreases, so the base case is never reached.
    #Correct. It must be `sum(n - 1)`. A base case only helps if each call moves towards it.
  ~ The base case should return 1 rather than 0.
    #Incorrect. The returned value does not affect whether the recursion terminates.
  ~ The comparison should be `<\=` rather than `\=\=`.
    #Incorrect. That would help for negative inputs, but the unchanged argument is the actual fault.
  ~ Recursive methods cannot return int.
    #Incorrect. They may return any type.
}

::t02_Q6:: You must walk a directory tree of unknown depth. Why is recursion a
natural fit here?
{
  = The data structure is itself recursive, so the code mirrors its shape and depth stays small.
    #Correct. A directory contains directories. An iterative version must maintain an explicit stack by hand.
  ~ Recursion is always faster than iteration for file access.
    #Incorrect. Speed is dominated by disk I/O, not by the traversal style.
  ~ Loops cannot access nested directories.
    #Incorrect. They can, using an explicit stack or queue.
  ~ Java requires recursion for filesystem traversal.
    #Incorrect. Files.walk and manual loops are both available.
}

::t02_Q7:: In a flood-fill algorithm, what corresponds to "making the problem smaller"?
{
  = Repainting the current cell so it no longer matches the target colour, then recursing into its neighbours.
    #Correct. Each repaint permanently shrinks the set of cells still eligible, which guarantees termination.
  ~ Reducing the size of the grid array on each call.
    #Incorrect. The grid is not resized; the set of matching cells shrinks instead.
  ~ Halving the number of neighbours visited each time.
    #Incorrect. All four neighbours are visited; progress comes from the repainting.
  ~ Decrementing a depth counter.
    #Incorrect. A depth limit would stop the recursion but would not correctly fill the region.
}

::t02_Q8:: When should you prefer iteration over recursion?
{
  = When the recursion depth could grow large enough to exhaust the call stack.
    #Correct. Java does not guarantee tail-call optimisation, so deep recursion risks StackOverflowError regardless of correctness.
  ~ Whenever the problem involves an array.
    #Incorrect. Binary search and merge sort are recursive and array-based.
  ~ Whenever performance matters at all.
    #Incorrect. The per-call overhead is usually negligible next to clarity.
  ~ Whenever the method returns a value.
    #Incorrect. Recursive methods commonly return values.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t03_ordering

::t03_Q1:: A class `Score` implements `Comparable<Score>`. Its `compareTo`
method returns `Integer.compare(other.value, this.value)` for the primary
comparison. After calling `list.sort(null)` on an ArrayList of Scores,
how are the elements ordered?
{
  = Scores are sorted in descending order by value (highest first).
    #Correct. Returning `Integer.compare(other.value, this.value)` reverses the natural order, placing larger values first.
  ~ Scores are sorted in ascending order by value (lowest first).
    #Incorrect. To get ascending order you would use `Integer.compare(this.value, other.value)`.
  ~ The sort order is undefined because `compareTo` returns the wrong sign.
    #Incorrect. The return value is well-defined; it consistently places higher values earlier.
  ~ A ClassCastException is thrown because `list.sort(null)` does not use Comparable.
    #Incorrect. Passing `null` as the comparator to `list.sort` uses the elements' natural order via Comparable.
}

::t03_Q2:: You have a `List<Product>` and want to sort by price ascending,
then by rating descending as a tie-breaker, using the Comparator factory API.
Which expression correctly expresses this?
{
  = `Comparator.comparingDouble(Product\:\:price).thenComparing(Comparator.comparingDouble(Product\:\:rating).reversed())`
    #Correct. `comparingDouble` on price gives ascending price order, and `.reversed()` on the rating comparator gives descending rating.
  ~ `Comparator.comparingDouble(Product\:\:price).thenComparing(Product\:\:rating)`
    #Incorrect. `thenComparing(Product\:\:rating)` sorts rating in ascending order, but descending is required.
  ~ `Comparator.comparingDouble(Product\:\:price).reversed().thenComparing(Product\:\:rating)`
    #Incorrect. Calling `.reversed()` on the price comparator gives descending price, not ascending.
  ~ `Comparator.comparing(Product\:\:price, Product\:\:rating)`
    #Incorrect. `Comparator.comparing` does not accept two key-extractor arguments in this form.
}

::t03_Q3:: Which statement best describes the difference between `Comparable`
and `Comparator` in Java?
{
  = `Comparable` defines the class's own natural ordering inside the class; `Comparator` defines an external ordering strategy without modifying the class.
    #Correct. `Comparable` is implemented by the class itself via `compareTo`, while `Comparator` is a separate object defining an alternative order.
  ~ `Comparable` is used only with arrays; `Comparator` is used only with collections.
    #Incorrect. Both can be used with arrays and collections; the distinction is about where the ordering logic lives.
  ~ `Comparator` is always faster than `Comparable` because it avoids casting.
    #Incorrect. Performance is not the distinction; both can be equally efficient.
  ~ `Comparable` allows multiple sort orders for the same class; `Comparator` allows only one.
    #Incorrect. It is the opposite: you can write many `Comparator` implementations for a class, but a class has only one `compareTo`.
}

::t03_Q4:: Java's `List.sort` is described as a *stable* sort. What does
stability mean in this context?
{
  = Elements that compare as equal keep their original relative order after sorting.
    #Correct. A stable sort preserves the prior ordering of elements that the comparator considers equal.
  ~ The sort always produces the same result when called multiple times on the same list.
    #Incorrect. This describes determinism, not stability; stability is specifically about equal elements preserving their order.
  ~ The sort does not modify the original list and instead returns a new sorted copy.
    #Incorrect. `List.sort` sorts in place; it does not return a new list.
  ~ The sort runs in O(n log n) time regardless of input order.
    #Incorrect. This describes time-complexity guarantees, which is a separate property from stability.
}


::t03_Q5:: [Predict the output] `List<String> names \= new ArrayList<>(List.of("bob", "Ann", "carl"));`
followed by `Collections.sort(names);` then printing the list. What is the order?
{
  = [Ann, bob, carl]
    #Correct. String's natural order compares by Unicode value, and all uppercase letters sort before all lowercase ones.
  ~ [Ann, bob, carl] only if a case-insensitive Comparator is supplied
    #Incorrect. This IS the default result. A case-insensitive comparator would be needed to get [Ann, bob, carl] from different input, not this.
  ~ [bob, Ann, carl]
    #Incorrect. That is the original insertion order, not the sorted order.
  ~ [carl, bob, Ann]
    #Incorrect. That is descending order; `Collections.sort` uses ascending natural order.
}

::t03_Q6:: [Spot the bug] A Comparator is written as
`(a, b) -> (int) (a.getPrice() - b.getPrice())` where `getPrice()` returns `double`.
Why is this unreliable?
{
  = The cast truncates the difference, so prices differing by less than 1.0 compare as equal.
    #Correct. A difference of 0.5 casts to 0, meaning "equal". Use `Double.compare(a.getPrice(), b.getPrice())` instead.
  ~ Lambdas cannot be used as Comparators.
    #Incorrect. Comparator is a functional interface, so a lambda is the normal way to write one.
  ~ The parameters must be explicitly typed for the comparator to compile.
    #Incorrect. Types are inferred from the target Comparator type.
  ~ Subtraction is not permitted inside a Comparator.
    #Incorrect. It is permitted but unsafe. With ints it also risks overflow, which is the same category of bug.
}

::t03_Q7:: You want products sorted by price ascending, then by rating descending.
Which expression is correct?
{
  = `Comparator.comparingDouble(Product::getPrice).thenComparing(Product::getRating, Comparator.reverseOrder())`
    #Correct. The reversal is applied to the rating key only, leaving the price ordering ascending.
  ~ `Comparator.comparingDouble(Product::getPrice).thenComparing(Product::getRating).reversed()`
    #Incorrect. `reversed()` applies to the whole chain, so price would become descending too.
  ~ `Comparator.comparingDouble(Product::getPrice).reversed().thenComparing(Product::getRating)`
    #Incorrect. This reverses the price ordering, which is the opposite of what was asked.
  ~ `Comparator.comparing(Product::getPrice, Product::getRating)`
    #Incorrect. `comparing` takes a key extractor and optionally a Comparator, not two key extractors.
}

::t03_Q8:: A class implements `Comparable` such that `compareTo` returns 0 for two
objects that `equals` reports as different. What is the practical consequence?
{
  = A TreeSet will treat the two objects as duplicates and silently keep only one.
    #Correct. TreeSet and TreeMap use compareTo, not equals, to detect duplicates, so an inconsistent ordering loses elements.
  ~ The code will not compile.
    #Incorrect. Consistency with equals is a documented contract, not a compiler rule.
  ~ A HashSet will store only one of the two objects.
    #Incorrect. HashSet uses hashCode and equals, so it is unaffected by compareTo.
  ~ `Collections.sort` will throw IllegalArgumentException.
    #Incorrect. Sorting works; the inconsistency only causes trouble in sorted set and map implementations.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t04_equality_hashing

::t04_Q1:: Two `String` objects are created with `new String("hello")` and
compared using `\=\=`. The result is `false`. What does this demonstrate?
{
  = `\=\=` tests reference identity, not value equality; two distinct String objects can hold the same characters but occupy different memory locations.
    #Correct. `\=\=` returns true only when both references point to the exact same object, regardless of content.
  ~ The `String` class overrides `\=\=` to compare characters, so `false` means the strings differ.
    #Incorrect. `\=\=` cannot be overridden in Java; it always tests reference identity for objects.
  ~ Both objects hold the same value, so the result should be `true`; this indicates a Java bug.
    #Incorrect. This is correct Java behaviour. `\=\=` on objects checks identity, not character content.
  ~ Using `new String(...)` produces objects that are never equal because they bypass the string pool.
    #Incorrect. While `new String(...)` bypasses the string pool, the objects are still equal by `equals()`; `\=\=` simply tests if they are the same object.
}

::t04_Q2:: A class overrides `equals` to compare by an `id` field but does
not override `hashCode`. A developer stores two instances with the same `id`
in a `HashSet`. What is the most likely problem?
{
  = Both objects may be stored in the set as if they were distinct, because they may produce different hash codes and land in different buckets.
    #Correct. The equals/hashCode contract requires that equal objects share the same hash code. Without a matching `hashCode`, the set cannot find the duplicate.
  ~ A compile-time error occurs because `hashCode` must always be overridden alongside `equals`.
    #Incorrect. Java does not enforce this at compile time; the contract is a programming convention, not a language rule.
  ~ The `HashSet` uses `equals` exclusively, so duplicates are still correctly detected.
    #Incorrect. `HashSet` first uses `hashCode` to find the bucket, then uses `equals`; if hash codes differ, `equals` is never called for the comparison.
  ~ The program throws a `NullPointerException` when the second object is added.
    #Incorrect. The likely problem is a logical bug (duplicate entries), not a null pointer.
}

::t04_Q3:: You are implementing `hashCode` for a class with two fields: `int id`
and `String email`. Which implementation correctly applies the standard
31-multiplier pattern?
{
  = `int result = Objects.hash(id, email);`
    #Correct. `Objects.hash(...)` is the idiomatic Java way to combine field hashes using the 31-multiplier pattern internally.
  ~ `return id + email.hashCode();`
    #Incorrect. A plain sum without a multiplier produces many collisions because adding fields commutatively means different field combinations can produce the same sum.
  ~ `return id ^ email.hashCode();`
    #Incorrect. XOR is symmetric and frequently produces 0 for equal fields, leading to poor hash distribution.
  ~ `return System.identityHashCode(this);`
    #Incorrect. This returns the default identity-based hash, which breaks the equals/hashCode contract when `equals` compares fields.
}

::t04_Q4:: What is the primary purpose of the bucket index calculation
`index \= h mod tableSize` in a hash-based lookup structure?
{
  = It maps an arbitrarily large hash code integer to a valid position within the fixed-size internal array.
    #Correct. The modulo operation constrains an unbounded hash integer to the range 0..tableSize-1.
  ~ It ensures that no two objects can produce the same bucket index, preventing collisions.
    #Incorrect. The modulo operation does not prevent collisions; multiple hash codes can map to the same bucket index.
  ~ It converts the hash code to an alphabetical sort order for string keys.
    #Incorrect. Modulo arithmetic has nothing to do with alphabetical ordering.
  ~ It encrypts the hash code so that the original value cannot be recovered.
    #Incorrect. Modulo is a lossy arithmetic operation, not encryption; it is used purely to select a bucket index.
}


::t04_Q5:: [Predict the output] A `Point` class overrides `equals` but not `hashCode`.
Two equal points `p1` and `p2` are created, `p1` is added to a `HashSet`, and
`set.contains(p2)` is called. What is printed?
{
  = false, because the two objects almost certainly hash to different buckets.
    #Correct. `contains` uses hashCode first. With the default identity hash the lookup checks the wrong bucket and never reaches the equals test.
  ~ true, because equals returns true.
    #Incorrect. equals is only consulted after hashCode has selected a bucket. The wrong bucket is searched.
  ~ The code throws IllegalStateException.
    #Incorrect. Overriding equals without hashCode compiles and runs; it simply misbehaves.
  ~ true, because Java derives hashCode from equals automatically.
    #Incorrect. Java never derives one from the other. You must override both.
}

::t04_Q6:: Which statement about the equals/hashCode contract is correct?
{
  = If two objects are equal they must have the same hash code, but equal hash codes do not imply equality.
    #Correct. The implication runs one way only. Two unequal objects sharing a hash code is a legal collision.
  ~ If two objects have the same hash code they must be equal.
    #Incorrect. This reverses the contract. Collisions are unavoidable because hashCode returns a finite int.
  ~ Equal objects may have different hash codes provided equals is correct.
    #Incorrect. This breaks the contract and causes lookups in hashed collections to fail.
  ~ hashCode must return a unique value for every distinct object.
    #Incorrect. Uniqueness is impossible in general, and is not required. Good distribution is what matters.
}

::t04_Q7:: [Spot the bug] An object is added to a `HashSet`, then a field used by its
`hashCode` is modified. `set.contains(obj)` now returns false. Why?
{
  = The object was filed under its original hash code and is now in the wrong bucket for its current one.
    #Correct. This is why fields used in hashCode should be immutable. The object is in the set but effectively unreachable, and cannot even be removed.
  ~ HashSet copies objects on insertion, so the original is unaffected.
    #Incorrect. Collections store references, not copies.
  ~ The set was silently emptied when the field changed.
    #Incorrect. The element is still present; only the lookup path is broken.
  ~ contains compares by reference, so it fails after any mutation.
    #Incorrect. contains uses hashCode then equals, not reference identity.
}

::t04_Q8:: Why is a Java `record` often the best choice for a value type such as a DTO?
{
  = It generates a correct equals, hashCode and toString from the components, and its fields are final.
    #Correct. This removes the commonest source of contract bugs and gives immutability, which is exactly what hashed collections need.
  ~ Records are faster at runtime than ordinary classes.
    #Incorrect. There is no general speed advantage; the benefit is correctness and brevity.
  ~ Records can be modified after construction, unlike classes.
    #Incorrect. The reverse is true. Record components are final.
  ~ Records do not require a constructor.
    #Incorrect. A canonical constructor is generated automatically, and you can supply a compact one to add validation.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t05_collections_1

::t05_Q1:: You call `names.remove("Maya")` on an `ArrayList<String>`. What
does this method do?
{
  = It removes the first occurrence of the String "Maya" from the list by value equality.
    #Correct. `remove(Object o)` scans from the start and removes the first element for which `equals` returns true.
  ~ It removes the element at index 5 because "Maya" has 4 characters and indexing starts at 1.
    #Incorrect. The String overload of `remove` removes by value, not by index.
  ~ It removes all occurrences of "Maya" from the list.
    #Incorrect. Only the first occurrence is removed; subsequent occurrences remain.
  ~ It throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException if "Maya" is not found in the list.
    #Incorrect. `remove(Object)` returns `false` if the element is not present; it does not throw.
}

::t05_Q2:: Code inside an enhanced for-each loop calls `list.remove(element)`
directly on the `ArrayList` while iterating. What is the likely outcome?
{
  = A ConcurrentModificationException is thrown at runtime.
    #Correct. Structurally modifying an ArrayList during enhanced for-each iteration invalidates the internal iterator and triggers this exception.
  ~ The element is removed silently and iteration continues from the next element.
    #Incorrect. This is the behaviour of `Iterator.remove()`, not direct list removal during for-each.
  ~ A compile-time error prevents this code from being written.
    #Incorrect. The code compiles without error; the exception only occurs at runtime.
  ~ The loop skips the removed element and processes the rest without any exception.
    #Incorrect. Java's fail-fast iterator detects the structural modification and throws ConcurrentModificationException.
}

::t05_Q3:: What is the time complexity of `ArrayList.get(i)` and
`ArrayList.contains(x)` respectively?
{
  = O(1) for `get` and O(n) for `contains`.
    #Correct. Random access by index is O(1) because ArrayList is backed by an array. `contains` must scan the whole list in the worst case.
  ~ O(n) for `get` and O(1) for `contains`.
    #Incorrect. Array-backed random access is O(1); linear scan for `contains` is O(n).
  ~ O(1) for both because ArrayList is backed by a hash table.
    #Incorrect. ArrayList is backed by a plain array, not a hash table. Only hash-based structures offer O(1) membership tests.
  ~ O(log n) for both because ArrayList keeps its elements sorted.
    #Incorrect. ArrayList does not maintain sorted order; elements are stored in insertion order.
}

::t05_Q4:: You want to safely remove from an ArrayList all elements that
start with the letter "A" while iterating. Which approach is correct?
{
  = Obtain an `Iterator` from the list and call `it.remove()` after each matching `it.next()`.
    #Correct. Using `Iterator.remove()` is the safe way to delete elements during iteration without causing ConcurrentModificationException.
  ~ Use an enhanced for-each loop and call `list.remove(element)` for each match.
    #Incorrect. This causes ConcurrentModificationException because the list is structurally modified during for-each iteration.
  ~ Use an index loop counting down from `list.size()-1` to `0` and call `list.remove(i)` for each match.
    #Incorrect. A reverse-index loop can work for some cases but the Iterator approach is cleaner and the standard Java idiom.
  ~ Call `list.clear()` and re-add only the non-matching elements into a new list.
    #Incorrect. This is unnecessarily destructive and replaces contents rather than selectively removing matches.
}


::t05_Q5:: [Spot the bug] This loop is meant to remove blank entries but throws
ConcurrentModificationException: `for (String s : list) \{ if (s.isBlank()) list.remove(s); \}`
What is the correct fix?
{
  = Use `list.removeIf(String::isBlank)`, or an explicit Iterator and call `it.remove()`.
    #Correct. The removal must go through the iterator so its internal modification count stays consistent with the list.
  ~ Change the enhanced for loop to a standard indexed for loop and keep `list.remove(s)`.
    #Incorrect. That avoids the exception but skips elements, because every removal shifts the remaining items left.
  ~ Wrap the loop body in a try/catch for ConcurrentModificationException.
    #Incorrect. Catching it hides a real bug and leaves the removal incomplete.
  ~ Synchronise the loop, since the exception name indicates a threading problem.
    #Incorrect. The name is misleading. No second thread is involved; the iterator detected a structural change made behind its back.
}

::t05_Q6:: [Predict the output] What does `list.remove(1)` do when
`List<Integer> list \= new ArrayList<>(List.of(10, 20, 30));`?
{
  = Removes the element at index 1, leaving [10, 30].
    #Correct. `remove(int)` removes by index. To remove the value 1 you would need `remove(Integer.valueOf(1))`.
  ~ Removes the value 1, leaving the list unchanged since 1 is not present.
    #Incorrect. That is `remove(Object)`. An `int` literal selects the index overload.
  ~ Throws IndexOutOfBoundsException.
    #Incorrect. Index 1 is valid for a three-element list.
  ~ Removes the first element, leaving [20, 30].
    #Incorrect. That would be `remove(0)`.
}

::t05_Q7:: Why is `ArrayList` usually a better default than `LinkedList` even for
frequent middle insertions?
{
  = Its elements are contiguous in memory, so bulk shifting is fast, whereas LinkedList must first walk the chain to reach the position.
    #Correct. `add(i, x)` on a LinkedList is O(n) to locate the node, and its scattered nodes defeat CPU caching.
  ~ ArrayList insertion in the middle is O(1).
    #Incorrect. It is O(n) because subsequent elements shift, but the shift is a fast bulk memory move.
  ~ LinkedList does not support insertion at an index.
    #Incorrect. It does; the operation is simply not as cheap as its O(1) reputation suggests.
  ~ ArrayList never needs to resize.
    #Incorrect. It resizes by copying to a larger array, though this is amortised and rare.
}

::t05_Q8:: A method should return "no results" as an empty collection. Which return
value is preferred, and why?
{
  = An empty `List`, because callers can iterate it without a null check.
    #Correct. Returning null forces every caller to guard, and a forgotten guard is a NullPointerException. Prefer `Collections.emptyList()` or `new ArrayList<>()`.
  ~ null, because it uses less memory than an empty list.
    #Incorrect. The saving is negligible and the cost is a class of avoidable bugs.
  ~ `Optional<List<T>>`, to signal absence explicitly.
    #Incorrect. An empty list already means "nothing". This gives two ways to express the same state.
  ~ A list containing a single null element.
    #Incorrect. This is worse than null, because the emptiness is now hidden inside the iteration.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t06_collections_2

::t06_Q1:: You have a `LinkedList<Integer>` and need to iterate forward,
inserting a marker element after every even number encountered.
Which iterator type allows safe insertion during the traversal?
{
  = `ListIterator`, which supports `it.add(element)` to insert after the last returned element.
    #Correct. `ListIterator` is the only Java iterator that supports both `add` and bidirectional traversal during iteration.
  ~ A standard `Iterator`, which already supports `add` via its `add` method.
    #Incorrect. `Iterator` only has `remove`; it does not have an `add` method.
  ~ An enhanced for-each loop with `list.add` called on the list directly.
    #Incorrect. Calling `list.add` directly during for-each causes ConcurrentModificationException.
  ~ An index-based for loop using `list.get(i)` to check each element.
    #Incorrect. `LinkedList.get(i)` is O(n) per call, making this approach inefficient; also it does not handle concurrent structural modification cleanly.
}

::t06_Q2:: A `LinkedList` is used as a `Deque`. Code calls `tasks.offer("load")`
followed by `tasks.offer("parse")` and then `tasks.poll()`. What does `poll()`
return?
{
  = "load", because `offer` enqueues at the tail and `poll` dequeues from the head (FIFO).
    #Correct. `offer` adds to the tail and `poll` removes from the head, implementing FIFO queue behaviour.
  ~ "parse", because `offer` enqueues at the head and `poll` dequeues from the head (LIFO).
    #Incorrect. `offer` adds to the tail, not the head. LIFO behaviour is obtained with `push`/`pop`.
  ~ null, because `poll` always returns null on a non-empty deque.
    #Incorrect. `poll` returns null only when the deque is empty; here it returns the first element.
  ~ An exception, because `Deque` does not support `offer` and `poll`.
    #Incorrect. `offer` and `poll` are the standard null-safe queue operations defined on `Deque`.
}

::t06_Q3:: Compared to `ArrayList`, what is the primary performance advantage
of `LinkedList` when you already hold a `ListIterator` at the point of change?
{
  = Insertions and removals at the current iterator position are O(1) because no elements need to shift.
    #Correct. LinkedList nodes are linked by references, so inserting or removing at a known node is O(1) pointer manipulation.
  ~ Random access via `get(i)` is O(1) because each node stores its own index.
    #Incorrect. `LinkedList.get(i)` is O(n); there is no index stored in nodes.
  ~ Memory usage is lower because LinkedList does not allocate extra capacity like ArrayList.
    #Incorrect. LinkedList uses more memory per element because each node stores two reference fields (next, previous) in addition to the value.
  ~ Sorting a LinkedList is faster than sorting an ArrayList.
    #Incorrect. Sorting a LinkedList is generally slower because it lacks the cache-friendly contiguous memory layout of an array.
}

::t06_Q4:: Why is it recommended to use `offer`, `poll`, and `peek` instead
of `add`, `remove`, and `element` when using a `Deque` as a queue?
{
  = `offer`, `poll`, and `peek` return null (or false) on failure rather than throwing exceptions, making them safer for queue operations.
    #Correct. The null-returning variants avoid exceptions when the queue is empty or full, which is more appropriate for conditional processing.
  ~ `offer`, `poll`, and `peek` operate at the tail of the deque, whereas `add`, `remove`, and `element` operate at the head.
    #Incorrect. Both sets of methods work with the head for removal/peek; the distinction is about exception behaviour, not position.
  ~ `add`, `remove`, and `element` are deprecated and should not be used in modern Java.
    #Incorrect. These methods are not deprecated; the difference is their error-handling behaviour.
  ~ `offer` and `poll` are faster because they bypass synchronisation checks.
    #Incorrect. Neither set of methods involves synchronisation; the distinction is about how they handle edge cases.
}


::t06_Q5:: Which operation is genuinely O(1) on a `LinkedList` but O(n) on an `ArrayList`?
{
  = Removing the first element.
    #Correct. LinkedList unlinks the head node directly, whereas ArrayList must shift every remaining element left.
  ~ Retrieving the element at index 500.
    #Incorrect. That is O(1) for ArrayList and O(n) for LinkedList, which must walk the chain.
  ~ Appending to the end.
    #Incorrect. Both are effectively O(1); ArrayList is amortised O(1) because resizing is rare.
  ~ Checking whether a value is present.
    #Incorrect. Both are O(n) linear scans.
}

::t06_Q6:: Which `ListIterator` capability is unavailable on a plain `Iterator`?
{
  = Replacing the current element with `set(...)` and walking backwards with `previous()`.
    #Correct. ListIterator adds set, add, previous and hasPrevious. A plain Iterator offers only hasNext, next and remove.
  ~ Removing the current element.
    #Incorrect. `remove()` exists on the plain Iterator too.
  ~ Iterating forwards through the collection.
    #Incorrect. Both do this; it is the basic purpose of an Iterator.
  ~ Being used in an enhanced for loop.
    #Incorrect. The enhanced for loop uses a plain Iterator, not a ListIterator.
}

::t06_Q7:: [Predict the output] A `Deque<String> d \= new LinkedList<>();` receives
`d.push("a"); d.push("b"); d.push("c");` and then `d.pop()` is printed. What appears?
{
  = c
    #Correct. `push` inserts at the head and `pop` removes from the head, so a Deque used this way behaves as a LIFO stack.
  ~ a
    #Incorrect. That would be FIFO behaviour, which you get from `offer` plus `poll`.
  ~ b
    #Incorrect. "b" sits in the middle and is not at either end after "c" is pushed.
  ~ The code throws NoSuchElementException.
    #Incorrect. The deque holds three elements, so pop succeeds.
}

::t06_Q8:: Why are two `Deque` objects a cleaner basis for undo/redo than one list
plus a current-position index?
{
  = The most-recent-first access pattern is exactly what a stack provides, so no index invariant has to be maintained by hand.
    #Correct. Undo pops from one stack and pushes to the other. There is no index that can drift out of range or off by one.
  ~ A Deque can hold more elements than a List.
    #Incorrect. Both grow as needed; capacity is not the issue.
  ~ A List cannot store command objects.
    #Incorrect. It can. The difference is the bookkeeping the two designs demand.
  ~ Deque operations are thread-safe by default.
    #Incorrect. `LinkedList` and `ArrayDeque` are not synchronised.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t07_collections_3_set_map

::t07_Q1:: Why does `HashSet` depend on `equals` and `hashCode` when `ArrayList` largely does not?
{
  = Its purpose is to detect duplicates in constant time, which requires hashing to a bucket and then comparing within it.
    #Correct. ArrayList only stores in order, so add never needs to compare anything.
  ~ Because HashSet stores elements in sorted order.
    #Incorrect. That is TreeSet. HashSet has no defined iteration order.
  ~ Because ArrayList cannot contain objects.
    #Incorrect. It can hold any reference type.
  ~ Because HashSet stores copies rather than references.
    #Incorrect. All Java collections store references.
}

::t07_Q2:: You need fast membership testing over a million strings. Which collection is best?
{
  = HashSet
    #Correct. Membership is O(1) on average, against O(n) for a List and O(log n) for a TreeSet.
  ~ ArrayList
    #Incorrect. `contains` is a linear scan, so a million lookups become prohibitively slow.
  ~ TreeSet
    #Incorrect. It works but costs O(log n) and only earns that if sorted order is also needed.
  ~ LinkedList
    #Incorrect. Membership testing is O(n), and it is slower than ArrayList in practice.
}

::t07_Q3:: When is `TreeMap` the right choice over `HashMap`?
{
  = When keys must be iterated in sorted order or queried by range.
    #Correct. TreeMap adds firstKey, headMap, tailMap, floorKey and ceilingKey, which HashMap cannot offer.
  ~ When lookups must be as fast as possible.
    #Incorrect. HashMap is O(1) average; TreeMap is O(log n).
  ~ When keys may be null.
    #Incorrect. The reverse: HashMap permits one null key, TreeMap generally does not.
  ~ When insertion order must be preserved.
    #Incorrect. That is LinkedHashMap. TreeMap uses sorted order, not insertion order.
}

::t07_Q4:: [Predict the output] Two distinct objects return the same `hashCode()` but
are not equal. Both are added to a HashSet. What is the size afterwards?
{
  = 2, because they collide into one bucket but equals distinguishes them.
    #Correct. Collisions are normal and legal; only lookup speed within that bucket is affected.
  ~ 1, because equal hash codes mean the objects are duplicates.
    #Incorrect. That reverses the contract. Equal hash codes do not imply equality.
  ~ 0, because the collision causes both insertions to fail.
    #Incorrect. Collisions are handled transparently by the implementation.
  ~ The add call throws IllegalStateException.
    #Incorrect. No exception occurs.
}

::t07_Q5:: Which expression counts word occurrences most idiomatically?
{
  = `counts.merge(word, 1, Integer::sum)`
    #Correct. It inserts 1 on first sight and otherwise adds to the existing value, in one call.
  ~ `counts.put(word, counts.get(word) + 1)`
    #Incorrect. This throws NullPointerException on the first occurrence, when get returns null.
  ~ `counts.putIfAbsent(word, counts.get(word) + 1)`
    #Incorrect. It has the same null problem and does not increment on later occurrences.
  ~ `counts.computeIfAbsent(word, k -> k.length())`
    #Incorrect. This stores the word length once and never counts anything.
}

::t07_Q6:: What ordering does a `PriorityQueue` guarantee?
{
  = Only that `poll()` returns the smallest remaining element according to its comparator.
    #Correct. Iterating or printing the queue directly does NOT yield sorted order, which surprises many students.
  ~ That iteration visits elements in fully sorted order.
    #Incorrect. The internal heap is only partially ordered.
  ~ That elements are returned in insertion order.
    #Incorrect. That would be an ordinary queue.
  ~ That the largest element is always returned first.
    #Incorrect. The default is smallest-first; a reversed comparator is needed for largest-first.
}

::t07_Q7:: [Spot the bug] A `HashMap<Player, Integer>` loses entries after the program
changes a Player's ID field. Why?
{
  = The key's hash code changed after insertion, so it is now looked up in the wrong bucket.
    #Correct. Fields used in hashCode must be immutable. The entry is present but effectively unreachable.
  ~ HashMap only supports String keys.
    #Incorrect. Any type with correct equals and hashCode may be a key.
  ~ The map silently rejects mutable objects.
    #Incorrect. It accepts them; the failure appears only on later lookups.
  ~ Changing a field removes the entry automatically.
    #Incorrect. The map is never notified of the change.
}

::t07_Q8:: Which Set implementation preserves the order in which elements were inserted?
{
  = LinkedHashSet
    #Correct. It keeps a linked list through the entries, giving predictable iteration at a small memory cost.
  ~ HashSet
    #Incorrect. Its iteration order is unspecified and may change.
  ~ TreeSet
    #Incorrect. It iterates in sorted order, which is not the same as insertion order.
  ~ All Set implementations preserve insertion order.
    #Incorrect. Only LinkedHashSet makes that guarantee.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t08_inheritance

::t08_Q1:: A subclass constructor does not explicitly call `super(...)`. The
parent class defines only a constructor that takes a String parameter and
no no-argument constructor. What happens at compile time?
{
  = A compile error occurs because Java automatically inserts `super()` (no-arg), which does not exist in the parent.
    #Correct. Without an explicit `super(...)` call, Java inserts `super()`. Since the parent has no no-arg constructor, this fails to compile.
  ~ The code compiles and the parent's String constructor is called with an empty string automatically.
    #Incorrect. Java inserts `super()` (no arguments), not `super("")`; and this fails if no no-arg constructor exists.
  ~ The subclass object is created without initialising any parent fields.
    #Incorrect. Java enforces constructor chaining; you cannot skip the parent constructor.
  ~ The parent class is bypassed entirely when the subclass constructor runs.
    #Incorrect. The parent constructor must always run; Java's constructor chaining rules ensure this.
}

::t08_Q2:: A method in a parent class is overridden in a subclass. An
`ArrayList<Parent>` holds a mix of parent and subclass objects. When the
loop calls `e.describe()` on each element, which version of `describe` is
executed for a subclass instance?
{
  = The subclass version of `describe`, determined at runtime based on the actual object type.
    #Correct. This is polymorphism: the JVM dispatches to the overriding method of the actual object, regardless of the reference type.
  ~ The parent version of `describe`, because the reference type is `Parent`.
    #Incorrect. The reference type controls which methods are visible at compile time, but the object type determines which implementation runs.
  ~ Both versions run in sequence, with the subclass appending to the parent's output.
    #Incorrect. Only one version runs unless the subclass explicitly calls `super.describe()`.
  ~ A ClassCastException is thrown because the list is typed as `ArrayList<Parent>`.
    #Incorrect. Storing subclass instances in a `List<Parent>` is valid polymorphism and does not cause a ClassCastException.
}

::t08_Q3:: What is the key reason to declare a class `abstract` rather than
making it a concrete class with default method bodies?
{
  = An abstract class can declare methods without implementation, forcing every subclass to provide its own version and preventing incomplete objects from being instantiated.
    #Correct. Abstract classes enforce a contract\: subclasses must implement abstract methods, and you cannot create an instance of the abstract class directly.
  ~ Abstract classes run faster because the JVM optimises abstract method dispatch.
    #Incorrect. Performance is not the reason to use abstract classes; the reason is design and contract enforcement.
  ~ Abstract classes allow a subclass to extend multiple parents simultaneously.
    #Incorrect. Java does not support multiple inheritance of classes, abstract or otherwise.
  ~ Declaring a class abstract prevents any subclass from overriding its methods.
    #Incorrect. It is the opposite: abstract classes invite overriding; `final` prevents it.
}

::t08_Q4:: What is the difference between method overriding and method overloading?
{
  = Overriding replaces a parent method with the same signature in a subclass; overloading provides multiple methods with the same name but different parameter lists in the same class.
    #Correct. Overriding is about runtime polymorphism across the inheritance hierarchy; overloading is a compile-time convenience within one class.
  ~ Overriding changes the return type of a method; overloading changes the parameter types.
    #Incorrect. Overriding must match the return type (or use a covariant return); overloading uses different parameter lists, not just return types.
  ~ Both terms describe the same concept but apply to different access modifiers.
    #Incorrect. They describe fundamentally different mechanisms: one is inheritance-based dispatch, the other is compile-time method resolution.
  ~ Overloading is only possible in abstract classes; overriding requires a concrete parent.
    #Incorrect. Overloading can occur in any class; overriding requires a method defined in a parent class or interface.
}


::t08_Q5:: [Spot the bug] A subclass defines `public boolean equals(Player other)`
but duplicate objects still appear in a `HashSet`. What went wrong?
{
  = The method overloads equals rather than overriding it, because the parameter must be `Object`.
    #Correct. Collections call `equals(Object)`, which is still the inherited identity version. Adding `@Override` would have made this a compile error.
  ~ equals cannot be overridden in a subclass.
    #Incorrect. It can and often should be, together with hashCode.
  ~ HashSet ignores equals and compares by reference.
    #Incorrect. HashSet uses hashCode then equals; here the wrong equals is being consulted.
  ~ The subclass must also declare `implements Comparable`.
    #Incorrect. Comparable governs ordering, not equality.
}

::t08_Q6:: [Predict the output] `Animal a \= new Dog();` where `Dog extends Animal`
and both define `speak()`. Which version runs, and why?
{
  = Dog's version, because instance method calls are dispatched on the runtime type.
    #Correct. This is polymorphism. The declared type governs which methods may be called; the runtime type governs which implementation executes.
  ~ Animal's version, because the variable is declared as Animal.
    #Incorrect. The declared type determines what the compiler permits, not which override runs.
  ~ Neither; the code does not compile without an explicit cast.
    #Incorrect. Assigning a subclass instance to a superclass reference is always allowed.
  ~ Dog's version only if `speak()` is declared abstract in Animal.
    #Incorrect. Overriding works for concrete parent methods too.
}

::t08_Q7:: A subclass constructor does not call `super(...)` explicitly. What happens?
{
  = Java inserts an implicit call to the parent's no-argument constructor, and compilation fails if none exists.
    #Correct. This is why a parent with only a parameterised constructor forces every subclass to call `super(...)` explicitly.
  ~ The parent constructor is skipped entirely.
    #Incorrect. A parent constructor always runs before the subclass body.
  ~ The parent's fields are left null or zero but the code still compiles.
    #Incorrect. If no no-arg constructor exists the code does not compile at all.
  ~ The compiler chooses whichever parent constructor best matches the subclass parameters.
    #Incorrect. There is no such matching. The implicit call is always to the no-argument constructor.
}

::t08_Q8:: A subclass overrides a method purely to throw `UnsupportedOperationException`.
What does this usually indicate?
{
  = The is-a relationship is wrong, and composition or a narrower interface would model it better.
    #Correct. If a child cannot honour the parent's contract it is not truly a kind of that parent. This violates the Liskov substitution principle.
  ~ It is good defensive practice for methods a subclass does not need.
    #Incorrect. It moves a design error into a runtime failure for anyone using the parent type.
  ~ The parent method should have been declared final.
    #Incorrect. That prevents the override but leaves the modelling problem unsolved.
  ~ The subclass is missing an @Override annotation.
    #Incorrect. The annotation is unrelated to the design problem being signalled.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t09_interface

::t09_Q1:: A Java class declaration reads `class Barrel implements Interactable, Damageable`. What does this tell you about the design?
{
  = `Barrel` commits to providing implementations for all methods declared in both `Interactable` and `Damageable`.
    #Correct. Implementing multiple interfaces means the class must supply concrete implementations for every abstract method from each interface.
  ~ `Barrel` inherits state (fields) from both `Interactable` and `Damageable`.
    #Incorrect. Interfaces cannot hold instance fields; `Barrel` inherits no state from them.
  ~ `Barrel` can only be used where a `Barrel` reference is expected, not through the interface types.
    #Incorrect. A `Barrel` instance can be assigned to variables of type `Interactable`, `Damageable`, or `Barrel`.
  ~ This is invalid Java because a class can implement at most one interface.
    #Incorrect. Java allows a class to implement any number of interfaces.
}

::t09_Q2:: An interface declares `int DEFAULT_INTERACTION_RANGE \= 2;`. What modifiers does this field implicitly have?
{
  = `public static final`.
    #Correct. All fields declared in an interface are implicitly `public static final`; they are compile-time constants shared across all implementations.
  ~ `protected static`.
    #Incorrect. Interface fields cannot be protected; they are always public and always final.
  ~ `private final`.
    #Incorrect. Interface fields must be public so implementing classes can access them.
  ~ No modifiers are applied; the field is treated as a regular instance variable.
    #Incorrect. Interface fields cannot be instance variables; they are always `public static final` constants.
}

::t09_Q3:: When should you prefer an interface over an abstract class?
{
  = When you want to define a behavioural capability that unrelated classes can share without requiring a common inheritance hierarchy.
    #Correct. Interfaces model "can-do" abilities and can be implemented by any class regardless of its place in the inheritance tree.
  ~ When you need to share concrete helper methods and private fields across a set of closely related classes.
    #Incorrect. Sharing implementation details and fields is the strength of an abstract class, not an interface.
  ~ When you want to prevent any class from instantiating the type directly.
    #Incorrect. Both abstract classes and interfaces cannot be instantiated directly; this is not the distinguishing criterion.
  ~ When you need to define a constructor that all implementing classes must call.
    #Incorrect. Interfaces cannot declare constructors; if you need a shared constructor chain, an abstract class is required.
}

::t09_Q4:: Code attempts to do `Interactable obj \= new Interactable();`.
What is the result?
{
  = A compile error, because interfaces cannot be instantiated directly.
    #Correct. You can only instantiate concrete classes that implement the interface, not the interface itself.
  ~ A runtime exception, because there is no class body to call the constructor on.
    #Incorrect. This is caught at compile time, not at runtime.
  ~ The code creates an empty object that responds to `interact()` with no operation.
    #Incorrect. Java does not create default empty implementations; the code will not compile.
  ~ The code is valid only if `Interactable` contains at least one default method.
    #Incorrect. Even with default methods, an interface cannot be directly instantiated.
}


::t09_Q5:: A class needs to be both drawable and serialisable, and to inherit shared
state from a base entity. What is the correct structure?
{
  = Extend the base class and implement both interfaces.
    #Correct. Java allows one superclass but any number of interfaces, which is exactly why capabilities are modelled as interfaces.
  ~ Extend all three, since Java supports multiple inheritance of classes.
    #Incorrect. Java permits only one superclass, specifically to avoid multiple-inheritance ambiguity.
  ~ Implement all three as interfaces and duplicate the shared state in each class.
    #Incorrect. Duplicating state is what the base class exists to prevent.
  ~ Create a chain where each interface extends the previous one.
    #Incorrect. This forces unrelated capabilities to be adopted together.
}

::t09_Q6:: What are the implicit modifiers on a field declared inside an interface?
{
  = public static final
    #Correct. Interface fields are constants, which is why they are written in upper case and cannot be reassigned.
  ~ private final
    #Incorrect. Interface fields are always public and accessible to every implementer.
  ~ public abstract
    #Incorrect. `abstract` applies to methods, not fields.
  ~ protected static
    #Incorrect. Interfaces have no protected members.
}

::t09_Q7:: When is an abstract class the better choice over an interface?
{
  = When implementers share common state and constructor logic, not merely a contract.
    #Correct. An abstract class can hold fields and a constructor that validates them; an interface cannot hold instance state.
  ~ When several unrelated classes need the same capability.
    #Incorrect. That is the case for an interface, since a class can implement many but extend only one.
  ~ When the type must define constants.
    #Incorrect. Both can define constants.
  ~ When you want to prevent the type from being instantiated.
    #Incorrect. Neither an abstract class nor an interface can be instantiated directly.
}

::t09_Q8:: [Spot the bug] A `Repository` interface is given twelve methods, and most
implementers leave half of them empty. What is the design fault?
{
  = The interface is too broad and should be split into smaller, focused ones.
    #Correct. This is the interface segregation principle. Empty implementations are the symptom that clients are being forced to depend on methods they do not use.
  ~ Interfaces are limited to a small number of methods in Java.
    #Incorrect. There is no language limit; the problem is one of design.
  ~ The methods should be given default implementations so nothing is empty.
    #Incorrect. Defaults can hide the problem, but the interface still bundles unrelated responsibilities.
  ~ The implementers should extend an abstract class instead.
    #Incorrect. That relocates the empty methods without reducing the interface's scope.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t10_exception_handling

::t10_Q1:: What distinguishes a checked exception from an unchecked one?
{
  = A checked exception must be caught or declared; an unchecked one carries no compiler obligation.
    #Correct. Checked types extend Exception but not RuntimeException, and model failures outside the program's control.
  ~ Checked exceptions are thrown at compile time.
    #Incorrect. All exceptions are thrown at runtime; only the requirement to handle them is enforced at compile time.
  ~ Unchecked exceptions cannot be caught.
    #Incorrect. They can be caught; you are simply not obliged to.
  ~ Checked exceptions are always more severe.
    #Incorrect. Severity is unrelated. The distinction is about who is expected to respond.
}

::t10_Q2:: Which pairing correctly classifies these exceptions?
{
  = IOException is checked; IllegalArgumentException is unchecked.
    #Correct. A failed file operation is environmental; a bad argument is a programming error.
  ~ Both are checked.
    #Incorrect. IllegalArgumentException extends RuntimeException and is unchecked.
  ~ Both are unchecked.
    #Incorrect. IOException extends Exception directly and is checked.
  ~ IOException is unchecked; IllegalArgumentException is checked.
    #Incorrect. This reverses both classifications.
}

::t10_Q3:: [Spot the bug] Production code contains `catch (Exception e) \{\}`. Name the two faults.
{
  = The catch is too broad and the body discards the error, so unrelated bugs vanish silently.
    #Correct. It swallows NullPointerException and every other unanticipated failure, leaving no trace of the cause.
  ~ Exception cannot be caught directly in Java.
    #Incorrect. It can, which is precisely why this compiles.
  ~ The catch block must declare a throws clause.
    #Incorrect. Catch blocks do not have throws clauses.
  ~ There is no fault provided the program continues running.
    #Incorrect. Continuing in an unknown state is the danger, not the remedy.
}

::t10_Q4:: What interface must a resource implement to be used in try-with-resources?
{
  = AutoCloseable
    #Correct. Closeable extends it for IO types. The resource is closed automatically on every exit path.
  ~ Serializable
    #Incorrect. That concerns object serialisation.
  ~ Comparable
    #Incorrect. That concerns ordering.
  ~ Runnable
    #Incorrect. That represents a task to execute.
}

::t10_Q5:: Why is try-with-resources safer than closing in a `finally` block?
{
  = If close() itself throws, the original exception is preserved and the close failure is attached as suppressed.
    #Correct. In the manual version the close exception replaces the original, hiding the real cause.
  ~ It closes resources faster.
    #Incorrect. Timing is not the benefit.
  ~ It prevents exceptions from being thrown inside the try block.
    #Incorrect. Exceptions still occur and still propagate.
  ~ finally blocks do not run when an exception is thrown.
    #Incorrect. They do run; the problem is what happens if close throws as well.
}

::t10_Q6:: When should a DAO `findById` return `Optional` rather than throw?
{
  = When "not found" is an expected, ordinary outcome rather than a failure.
    #Correct. Reserve exceptions for the exceptional. A dropped connection, by contrast, should still throw.
  ~ Always, since DAOs must never throw.
    #Incorrect. Genuine storage failures must not be flattened into an empty result.
  ~ Never, because a missing row is always an error.
    #Incorrect. Looking up an ID that does not exist is routine user behaviour.
  ~ Only when the method is declared static.
    #Incorrect. Unrelated to the decision.
}

::t10_Q7:: [Predict the output] A method has a `finally` block and the `try` block
executes `return 1;`. Does the finally block run?
{
  = Yes, it runs before the method actually returns.
    #Correct. finally executes on every exit path, including return, break and thrown exceptions.
  ~ No, return exits the method immediately.
    #Incorrect. The return value is computed, then finally runs, then control leaves the method.
  ~ Only if an exception was thrown.
    #Incorrect. It runs regardless.
  ~ Only if the method returns void.
    #Incorrect. The return type is irrelevant.
}

::t10_Q8:: In what order must catch blocks for `IOException` and `FileNotFoundException` appear?
{
  = FileNotFoundException first, because a supertype catch placed earlier would absorb it.
    #Correct. Catch blocks are tested top to bottom, so the most specific type must come first. The reverse order fails to compile.
  ~ IOException first, because it is checked.
    #Incorrect. Both are checked, and this order makes the second block unreachable.
  ~ The order is irrelevant.
    #Incorrect. Java rejects an unreachable catch block outright.
  ~ They must be combined into a single multi-catch.
    #Incorrect. Multi-catch is not permitted for types in a subclass relationship.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t11_generics_1

::t11_Q1:: A class stores its value as `private Object value`. A caller
retrieves the value and casts it to `Integer` but the stored object is actually
a `String`. What happens and when?
{
  = A ClassCastException is thrown at runtime when the cast is attempted.
    #Correct. Without generics, the compiler cannot verify the cast, so the type mismatch is only detected when the JVM executes the cast.
  ~ A compile error prevents this code from being written.
    #Incorrect. Casting to a specific type is always permitted at compile time; the compiler cannot know what the Object actually holds.
  ~ The String value is silently converted to an Integer.
    #Incorrect. Java does not implicitly convert between unrelated types.
  ~ The program returns null rather than throwing an exception.
    #Incorrect. A failed cast throws ClassCastException; it does not return null.
}

::t11_Q2:: A generic class is declared as `class Slot<T extends Item>`.
An instance is created as `Slot<Potion> potionSlot`. Which statement is true?
{
  = Attempting to call `potionSlot.put(new Sword(...))` causes a compile error, not a runtime error.
    #Correct. The type parameter `T` is resolved to `Potion` at compile time, so passing a `Sword` is rejected by the compiler.
  ~ The compiler permits `potionSlot.put(new Sword(...))` because `Sword` is also an `Item`.
    #Incorrect. The slot is parameterised as `Slot<Potion>`, so only `Potion` instances are accepted.
  ~ A runtime ClassCastException occurs when `put` is called with the wrong item type.
    #Incorrect. Generics catch type mismatches at compile time, preventing the ClassCastException from occurring at runtime.
  ~ The bound `T extends Item` means the slot can store any object, not just Items.
    #Incorrect. The bound restricts T to Item subtypes; it does not widen the accepted types.
}

::t11_Q3:: A generic method signature reads `public static <T> T first(List<T> items)`.
A caller invokes `first(List.of("A", "B"))`. What type does the method return,
and is a cast required?
{
  = The method returns a `String` and no cast is required because the type parameter is inferred as `String`.
    #Correct. The compiler infers `T` as `String` from the argument, so the return type is `String` and no cast is needed.
  ~ The method returns an `Object` and a cast to `String` is required.
    #Incorrect. Type inference allows the compiler to resolve `T` to `String`, eliminating the need for a cast.
  ~ A compile error occurs because generic methods cannot infer a return type from a list.
    #Incorrect. Java performs type inference automatically for generic method calls.
  ~ The method throws a ClassCastException at runtime when the result is used as a String.
    #Incorrect. With correct generics and type inference, no cast is involved and no ClassCastException can occur.
}

::t11_Q4:: Why is `new T()` not allowed inside a generic class body in Java?
{
  = Java generics use type erasure\: the type parameter T is replaced by `Object` at runtime, so the JVM has no information about which class to construct.
    #Correct. Type erasure removes generic type information at runtime, making `new T()` impossible because the JVM does not know T's concrete type.
  ~ Generic type parameters can only be used as method return types, not as constructor targets.
    #Incorrect. Type parameters can be used in many positions; the restriction on `new T()` is specifically due to type erasure.
  ~ It is a design decision to force developers to use abstract factories instead.
    #Incorrect. While a Supplier/factory is a common workaround, the underlying reason is the technical constraint of type erasure.
  ~ `new T()` is allowed when T extends a concrete class with a no-arg constructor.
    #Incorrect. Even with a bound, `new T()` is not permitted; a `Supplier<T>` or reflective approach is required.
}


::t11_Q5:: Why does `List<String>` and `List<Integer>` share a single class file at runtime?
{
  = Type erasure removes type arguments after compilation, leaving a raw List.
    #Correct. Generics are a compile-time device. This is why you cannot write `new T[]` or test `x instanceof List<String>`.
  ~ The JVM stores type arguments in the object header.
    #Incorrect. Java generics are erased, unlike C# where they are reified at runtime.
  ~ The compiler generates a separate class per type argument and discards duplicates.
    #Incorrect. That is C++ template instantiation, not Java.
  ~ Both are subclasses of a common List type parameterised at runtime.
    #Incorrect. No runtime parameterisation exists after erasure.
}

::t11_Q6:: [Spot the bug] A generic method declared `static <T> T max(List<T> items)`
fails to compile at the line `if (items.get(i).compareTo(best) > 0)`. Why?
{
  = `T` is unbounded, so the compiler only knows it is an Object and cannot guarantee compareTo exists.
    #Correct. Declare it `<T extends Comparable<T>>` so the bound supplies the capability being used.
  ~ Generic methods cannot call methods on their type parameter at all.
    #Incorrect. They can, provided a bound guarantees the method exists.
  ~ The method must be non-static to use a type parameter.
    #Incorrect. Static generic methods are permitted and common.
  ~ `compareTo` can only be called on primitive types.
    #Incorrect. It is defined on Comparable reference types; primitives do not have methods.
}

::t11_Q7:: [Predict the output] What happens when this compiles and runs:
`List<Object> objs \= new ArrayList<>(); objs.add("text"); objs.add(42);`
followed by `String s \= (String) objs.get(1);`
{
  = It compiles but throws ClassCastException at runtime.
    #Correct. The compiler permits the cast because the static type is Object. This is exactly the failure generics exist to prevent.
  ~ It fails to compile because an Integer cannot be cast to String.
    #Incorrect. The declared element type is Object, so the compiler cannot detect the mismatch.
  ~ It prints 42.
    #Incorrect. The cast is checked at runtime and fails before anything is assigned.
  ~ It returns null because the cast fails silently.
    #Incorrect. A failed cast throws rather than yielding null.
}

::t11_Q8:: What is the practical benefit of the diamond operator in
`Map<String, List<Integer>> m \= new HashMap<>();`?
{
  = The compiler infers the type arguments from the declaration, avoiding an error-prone repetition.
    #Correct. Repeating a long type invites a mismatch, and the inferred version stays correct if the declaration changes.
  ~ It creates a raw HashMap with no type checking.
    #Incorrect. That would be `new HashMap()` without the diamond, which does disable checking and produces a warning.
  ~ It allows the map to hold any key or value type at runtime.
    #Incorrect. The declared types are still enforced at compile time.
  ~ It makes the map immutable.
    #Incorrect. Immutability comes from `Map.of(...)` or `Collections.unmodifiableMap(...)`.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t12_generics_2

::t12_Q1:: Why is `List<Enemy>` not assignable to `List<Entity>` even though Enemy extends Entity?
{
  = Generics are invariant, which prevents inserting a different Entity subtype into a list others believe holds only Enemy.
    #Correct. Allowing it would let a Player be added, and a later get would throw ClassCastException.
  ~ Because Enemy does not really extend Entity at runtime.
    #Incorrect. The inheritance relationship holds; it simply does not propagate to the generic types.
  ~ Because List is an interface rather than a class.
    #Incorrect. Invariance applies to all generic types.
  ~ Because type erasure removes the relationship at compile time.
    #Incorrect. Erasure happens after type checking and is not the reason for invariance.
}

::t12_Q2:: What does PECS stand for, and what does it govern?
{
  = Producer Extends, Consumer Super - it decides which wildcard a parameter should use.
    #Correct. If you read from the parameter use extends; if you write into it use super.
  ~ Parameter Extends, Class Super - it governs class declarations.
    #Incorrect. PECS concerns wildcards on parameters, not class declarations.
  ~ Public Extends, Complete Super - a visibility rule.
    #Incorrect. It has nothing to do with access modifiers.
  ~ Primitive Extends, Collection Super - a boxing rule.
    #Incorrect. Wildcards do not apply to primitives at all.
}

::t12_Q3:: A method sums values it reads from a list. Which parameter type is correct?
{
  = `List<? extends Number>`
    #Correct. The list is a producer, so extends allows List<Integer>, List<Double> and List<Long> to be passed.
  ~ `List<? super Number>`
    #Incorrect. super is for parameters you write into, and would reject List<Integer>.
  ~ `List<Number>`
    #Incorrect. This accepts only an exact List<Number>, rejecting List<Integer>.
  ~ `List<?>`
    #Incorrect. Elements would come back as Object, so they could not be summed without casting.
}

::t12_Q4:: [Spot the bug] A method declared `void addAll(List<? extends T> dest, T... items)`
fails to compile at `dest.add(item)`. Why?
{
  = You cannot add to a `? extends T` list, because the exact element type is unknown.
    #Correct. The parameter is a consumer here, so it should be `List<? super T>`.
  ~ Varargs cannot be used with generics at all.
    #Incorrect. They can, though an unchecked warning about generic array creation may appear.
  ~ The method must be static.
    #Incorrect. Irrelevant to the error.
  ~ `add` does not exist on the List interface.
    #Incorrect. It does; the wildcard is what makes this call unsafe.
}

::t12_Q5:: Why is `Collections.copy` declared as
`copy(List<? super T> dest, List<? extends T> src)` rather than using `List<T>` for both?
{
  = So a source of a more specific type can be copied into a destination of a more general one.
    #Correct. With List<T> for both, copying a List<Integer> into a List<Number> would be rejected despite being entirely safe.
  ~ Because List<T> does not compile in a static method.
    #Incorrect. It compiles; it is simply more restrictive than necessary.
  ~ Because the destination must be immutable.
    #Incorrect. The destination is written to and must be mutable.
  ~ Because src and dest must be different classes.
    #Incorrect. They may be the same implementation type.
}

::t12_Q6:: What can you safely do with a variable of type `List<?>`?
{
  = Read elements as Object, and call methods that do not depend on the element type, such as size().
    #Correct. You cannot add anything except null, because the actual element type is unknown.
  ~ Add any object to it.
    #Incorrect. The compiler cannot verify the type is acceptable, so add is rejected.
  ~ Add only Strings to it.
    #Incorrect. No specific type is permitted.
  ~ Nothing at all; the type is unusable.
    #Incorrect. It is useful for read-only or type-agnostic operations.
}

::t12_Q7:: Why do many JDK methods accept `Comparator<? super T>` rather than `Comparator<T>`?
{
  = So a comparator written for a supertype can be reused to sort a list of subtypes.
    #Correct. A Comparator<Entity> can order a List<Enemy>, because a comparator consumes the elements it compares.
  ~ Because Comparator cannot be parameterised with T directly.
    #Incorrect. It can; `? super T` simply accepts more callers.
  ~ Because it allows the comparator to return a subtype.
    #Incorrect. compare always returns int.
  ~ Because it makes the sort algorithm faster.
    #Incorrect. It affects which arguments are accepted, not performance.
}

::t12_Q8:: Java arrays are covariant, so `Enemy[]` is an `Entity[]`. What is the consequence?
{
  = Storing the wrong subtype compiles but throws ArrayStoreException at runtime.
    #Correct. Generics chose invariance precisely to move this class of error to compile time.
  ~ The same protection as generics, enforced at compile time.
    #Incorrect. Arrays defer the check to runtime, which is strictly weaker.
  ~ Arrays cannot hold subclass instances.
    #Incorrect. They can, which is what covariance permits.
  ~ Array assignment between related types fails to compile.
    #Incorrect. It compiles; that is the problem.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t13_design_patterns_1

::t13_Q1:: The Strategy pattern is described as "define a family of algorithms, encapsulate each one, and make them interchangeable". In the notes example, what role does the `TaskRunner` class play?
{
  = Context — it holds a reference to the strategy interface and delegates execution to it without knowing the concrete implementation.
    #Correct. The context (TaskRunner) uses the strategy interface to perform work, enabling the behaviour to be changed by injecting a different strategy.
  ~ Strategy — it defines the algorithm that `ExecutionStrategy` implementations must follow.
    #Incorrect. `TaskRunner` delegates to the strategy; it does not define the algorithm. The strategy interface defines the contract.
  ~ Concrete Strategy — it provides one specific implementation of the execution algorithm.
    #Incorrect. `FastExecution` and `SafeExecution` are the concrete strategies; `TaskRunner` is the context.
  ~ Invoker — it enqueues commands and decides when to execute them.
    #Incorrect. The Invoker role belongs to the Command pattern (e.g. `CommandQueue`), not the Strategy pattern.
}

::t13_Q2:: A codebase has a method containing a large `switch` statement with one case per payment type\: CARD, PAYPAL, CRYPTO. Adding a new payment type requires editing this method. Which design problem does this illustrate, and which pattern addresses it?
{
  = The code violates the open/closed principle because adding behaviour requires modifying existing code; the Strategy pattern addresses this by encapsulating each payment algorithm in its own class.
    #Correct. Strategy replaces conditional-based variation with a family of interchangeable objects, allowing extension without modification.
  ~ The code has too many classes and should be simplified by moving all logic into a single method; no pattern is needed.
    #Incorrect. Consolidating all logic into one method increases coupling and makes future changes harder, not easier.
  ~ The Command pattern solves this by wrapping each switch case in a command object that can be queued.
    #Incorrect. Command addresses decoupling request invocation from execution; Strategy addresses varying the algorithm itself.
  ~ This is a normal use of polymorphism and no design pattern is required.
    #Incorrect. While polymorphism is involved, the recognised design solution for replacing conditionals over behaviour variants is the Strategy pattern.
}

::t13_Q3:: In the Command pattern, what is the main benefit of encapsulating a request as an object?
{
  = The request can be stored, queued, logged, or executed at a later time without the sender needing to know the details of the action.
    #Correct. Turning a request into an object decouples the sender (invoker) from the receiver, enabling deferred or batched execution.
  ~ The request runs faster because it avoids the overhead of method call dispatch.
    #Incorrect. Performance is not the motivation for the Command pattern.
  ~ The request automatically retries on failure without any additional code.
    #Incorrect. Retry logic is not built into the Command pattern; it must be added explicitly if needed.
  ~ It prevents any other code from modifying the request after it has been created.
    #Incorrect. Immutability of command objects is a design choice, not an inherent property of the pattern.
}

::t13_Q4:: A `CommandQueue` has a `Queue<Command>` field and calls
`command.execute()` in a loop. Which Statement accurately describes the
relationship between the queue and the concrete command implementations?
{
  = The queue is decoupled from the concrete commands; it only depends on the `Command` interface, so new command types can be added without modifying the queue.
    #Correct. Programming to the interface means the queue needs no knowledge of what each command does, satisfying the open/closed principle.
  ~ The queue must be rewritten whenever a new concrete command class is added.
    #Incorrect. Because the queue uses the `Command` interface, new implementations can be added without touching the queue code.
  ~ The queue and commands must be in the same package to allow `execute()` to be called.
    #Incorrect. Access control, not package membership, determines visibility; `execute()` is public on the interface.
  ~ Concrete commands must extend the `CommandQueue` class to be eligible for processing.
    #Incorrect. Commands implement the `Command` interface; they have no inheritance relationship with the queue.
}


::t13_Q5:: Which symptom most strongly suggests Strategy should replace existing code?
{
  = An if/else or switch chain selecting between algorithms, which is edited every time a new option is added.
    #Correct. Strategy makes each option a class, so a new option is a new file rather than a change to working code.
  ~ A class with a large number of fields.
    #Incorrect. That suggests the class has too many responsibilities, not that an algorithm needs swapping.
  ~ A method with more than three parameters.
    #Incorrect. That points towards introducing a parameter object.
  ~ Several classes implementing the same interface.
    #Incorrect. That is normal and often desirable.
}

::t13_Q6:: Why must a Command capture the data it needs at construction rather than
reading it during `execute()`?
{
  = Execution may be deferred, so the surrounding state could have changed by the time it runs.
    #Correct. Commands are queued, logged, replayed or undone. Reading current state at execution time would apply the action to the wrong data.
  ~ Because `execute()` is not allowed to read fields.
    #Incorrect. It reads its own fields; the point is when those values were captured.
  ~ Because constructors run faster than methods.
    #Incorrect. Performance is irrelevant here.
  ~ Because the Command interface forbids parameters on execute().
    #Incorrect. The no-argument signature is a consequence of self-containment, not the reason for it.
}

::t13_Q7:: To support undo, what must a `ChangeStatusCommand` store in addition to the new status?
{
  = The previous status, captured before the change is applied.
    #Correct. Undo restores the prior value, so it must be read in the constructor rather than at undo time.
  ~ Only the new status, since undo can recalculate the old one.
    #Incorrect. The old value is not generally recoverable from the new one.
  ~ A reference to the undo stack.
    #Incorrect. The invoker owns the stack; a command that knew about it would be needlessly coupled.
  ~ A timestamp of when execute() was called.
    #Incorrect. Useful for logging, but not required to reverse the change.
}

::t13_Q8:: [Spot the bug] Each concrete Strategy is created with `new` inside the
context class. What benefit of the pattern has been lost?
{
  = The context is still coupled to every concrete strategy, so adding one means editing it.
    #Correct. The strategy should be injected through the constructor or a setter, or supplied by a factory.
  ~ Nothing; strategies must be created by the context.
    #Incorrect. Creating them there reintroduces exactly the dependency the pattern removes.
  ~ The strategies can no longer implement a common interface.
    #Incorrect. They still can; the problem is who constructs them.
  ~ Polymorphic dispatch stops working.
    #Incorrect. Dispatch still works; the design has simply not been decoupled.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t14_design_patterns_2

::t14_Q1:: The Factory pattern's intent is to "encapsulate object creation so
clients depend on what they want, not how it is built." What is the primary
problem it solves?
{
  = It removes object creation logic from client code, centralising it so that changes to how objects are built do not ripple through every caller.
    #Correct. When clients call `factory.createFor(task)` instead of `new ExecuteTaskCommand(task, new ValidatedExecution())`, only the factory needs updating if the construction changes.
  ~ It makes all objects immutable so they cannot be changed after creation.
    #Incorrect. The Factory pattern is about controlling creation, not enforcing immutability.
  ~ It ensures only one instance of each class is ever created throughout the application.
    #Incorrect. That is the Singleton pattern; Factory creates new instances as requested.
  ~ It prevents subclasses from overriding the creation logic defined in the parent.
    #Incorrect. Factory Method pattern actually encourages subclasses to override creation; the goal is decoupling clients from construction details.
}

::t14_Q2:: In the Observer pattern, an `ObservableTaskQueue` notifies a list
of `TaskListener` objects after each command executes. What key design property
does this provide?
{
  = The queue does not need to know what listeners do; listeners can be added or removed without modifying the queue.
    #Correct. Observer decouples the subject (queue) from its observers (listeners), allowing the set of reactions to change independently.
  ~ All listeners are guaranteed to execute before the next command is dequeued.
    #Incorrect. The order and timing of notifications depends on implementation; the pattern does not guarantee sequential completion.
  ~ The queue and listeners share the same thread, preventing concurrency issues.
    #Incorrect. Thread safety is a separate concern; Observer does not inherently guarantee single-threaded execution.
  ~ Listeners are notified only if they hold a direct reference to the task that changed.
    #Incorrect. The subject notifies all registered listeners; they do not need to hold direct references to specific tasks.
}

::t14_Q3:: An `ExternalJobAdapter` implements the `Command` interface and
wraps an `ExternalJob` whose only method is `runJob()`. What structural role
does this class play and why is it used?
{
  = It is an Adapter that bridges the incompatible `ExternalJob` interface to the `Command` interface expected by the system.
    #Correct. The adapter translates `execute()` calls into `runJob()` calls, allowing the external job to work within the existing command infrastructure.
  ~ It is a Facade that simplifies a complex subsystem into a single unified interface.
    #Incorrect. A Facade simplifies multiple complex components; an Adapter bridges one incompatible interface to another.
  ~ It is a Decorator that adds new behaviour around the original `ExternalJob` method.
    #Incorrect. A Decorator adds responsibilities to an object; an Adapter converts its interface without adding new behaviour.
  ~ It is a Proxy that controls access to the `ExternalJob` for security purposes.
    #Incorrect. A Proxy controls or logs access to an object; an Adapter is used purely for interface compatibility.
}

::t14_Q4:: When comparing the Observer and Command patterns, which statement
best distinguishes their primary concerns?
{
  = Command encapsulates a unit of work as an object so it can be queued or deferred; Observer propagates a state-change notification to multiple dependents automatically.
    #Correct. Command is about representing work; Observer is about broadcasting events to listeners without tight coupling.
  ~ Command is used for UI events and Observer is used for database transactions.
    #Incorrect. Neither pattern is domain-specific; both appear in UI, databases, games, and many other contexts.
  ~ Observer requires the subject to know all observer types at compile time; Command does not.
    #Incorrect. Observer depends on an interface (e.g. `TaskListener`), so the subject does not need to know concrete observer types.
  ~ Both patterns achieve exactly the same decoupling and are interchangeable.
    #Incorrect. They solve different problems\: one manages work execution, the other manages event propagation.
}


::t14_Q5:: What is the principal benefit of a factory over calling `new` directly at each site?
{
  = The concrete class is named in exactly one place, so implementations can be swapped without editing callers.
    #Correct. Callers depend only on the interface, which also makes substituting a test implementation straightforward.
  ~ Object creation becomes faster.
    #Incorrect. There is no performance benefit; the gain is in coupling.
  ~ It removes the need for constructors.
    #Incorrect. The factory calls a constructor; it just centralises that call.
  ~ It guarantees only one instance is ever created.
    #Incorrect. That is Singleton, a different pattern.
}

::t14_Q6:: Which problem does Observer solve that Strategy does not?
{
  = Notifying an unknown number of interested parties when something happens, without the publisher knowing who they are.
    #Correct. Observer is one-to-many notification. Strategy is a single interchangeable algorithm chosen by the context.
  ~ Selecting between alternative algorithms at runtime.
    #Incorrect. That is precisely what Strategy does.
  ~ Making an incompatible interface usable.
    #Incorrect. That is Adapter.
  ~ Deferring object creation to a subclass.
    #Incorrect. That is Factory Method.
}

::t14_Q7:: When is Adapter preferable to refactoring the mismatched class?
{
  = When the class cannot be changed, such as third-party or legacy code others depend on.
    #Correct. The adapter isolates the mismatch in one class, so an external API change affects a single file.
  ~ Whenever two interfaces differ, since adapters are always cheaper.
    #Incorrect. Accumulating adapters over code you own hides a design problem you could fix properly.
  ~ When the two interfaces are already identical.
    #Incorrect. No adapter is needed in that case.
  ~ When you want to add new behaviour to the class.
    #Incorrect. Adapter translates an existing interface; adding behaviour is Decorator.
}

::t14_Q8:: [Spot the bug] An Observer subject holds subscribers in a plain `ArrayList`,
and a subscriber unregisters itself while being notified. What is the likely result?
{
  = ConcurrentModificationException, because the list is modified during iteration over it.
    #Correct. Iterate a copy of the list, or use CopyOnWriteArrayList, which is designed for this listener pattern.
  ~ The subscriber is silently ignored and iteration completes normally.
    #Incorrect. The iterator detects the structural change and fails fast.
  ~ The subject deadlocks.
    #Incorrect. Deadlock requires competing locks; this is a single-threaded structural modification.
  ~ Nothing, because ArrayList permits modification during iteration.
    #Incorrect. It explicitly does not, which is what the fail-fast iterator enforces.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t15_dao

::t15_Q1:: In an N-tier architecture using the DAO pattern, which layer should
contain SQL statements and JDBC code?
{
  = The data access layer (DAO) only — SQL must not appear in the service layer or the presentation layer.
    #Correct. The DAO pattern isolates all persistence logic in one place, keeping the service and presentation layers free of SQL.
  ~ The service layer, so that business rules and data retrieval logic are co-located.
    #Incorrect. Mixing SQL with business rules defeats the purpose of the DAO pattern and makes the system harder to test and maintain.
  ~ The presentation (UI) layer, because it decides which data to display.
    #Incorrect. The UI should never contain SQL; it should call service methods and display the results.
  ~ Any layer, because JDBC is a utility library with no architectural constraints.
    #Incorrect. While technically possible, scattering JDBC code throughout layers is a known design smell that the DAO pattern was created to prevent.
}

::t15_Q2:: Why should a JDBC DAO always use `PreparedStatement` with `?`
placeholders rather than building SQL strings by concatenation?
{
  = String concatenation of user input into SQL creates a SQL injection vulnerability; `PreparedStatement` sends parameters separately, preventing malicious input from altering the query structure.
    #Correct. SQL injection is one of the most serious security vulnerabilities; `PreparedStatement` is the standard defence.
  ~ `PreparedStatement` is faster than string concatenation in all cases.
    #Incorrect. While prepared statements can be faster due to pre-compilation, the primary reason to use them is security, not performance.
  ~ String concatenation causes a `NullPointerException` when any parameter is null.
    #Incorrect. String concatenation handles nulls without throwing; the critical issue is security, not null handling.
  ~ Java's compiler rejects SQL string concatenation at compile time.
    #Incorrect. The compiler cannot distinguish SQL strings from other strings; SQL injection is a runtime security issue.
}

::t15_Q3:: A `TaskDao` interface is defined with methods like `findById`,
`insert`, and `deleteById`. The service layer depends on `TaskDao`, not on
`JdbcTaskDao`. What design benefit does this provide?
{
  = The persistence implementation can be swapped (e.g. from JDBC to an in-memory DAO for testing) without changing the service layer.
    #Correct. Programming to the interface decouples the service from any specific database technology, enabling easy testing and future migration.
  ~ It prevents the DAO from being instantiated more than once, acting like a singleton.
    #Incorrect. The interface type has no effect on instantiation; the factory or constructor controls object lifecycle.
  ~ The interface guarantees that all DAO methods will execute within a database transaction.
    #Incorrect. Transactions must be explicitly managed; the interface defines the method contract, not transactional behaviour.
  ~ It allows the service layer to call private DAO methods directly.
    #Incorrect. An interface only exposes public methods; private implementation details remain hidden in the implementing class.
}

::t15_Q4:: A DAO method returns `Optional<Task>` rather than `Task` or `null`.
What does calling `dao.findById(999).orElseThrow(...)` express?
{
  = The caller expects the task to exist and treats its absence as an exceptional condition worth an exception.
    #Correct. `orElseThrow` is the idiom for "this must be present; if not, throw a meaningful exception rather than a NullPointerException later".
  ~ The method returns the default Task object when no row is found.
    #Incorrect. `orElseThrow` throws an exception on empty; `orElse(defaultValue)` would provide a default.
  ~ The DAO always finds a result when `orElseThrow` is called, so the Optional is always present.
    #Incorrect. `orElseThrow` throws if the Optional is empty; it does not guarantee the Optional is present.
  ~ Using `Optional` means null is never returned, so `orElseThrow` is unnecessary.
    #Incorrect. An empty `Optional` is the representation of "no value"; `orElseThrow` is needed to explicitly handle that case.
}


::t15_Q5:: Why must user-supplied values be bound with `?` placeholders on a
`PreparedStatement` rather than concatenated into the SQL string?
{
  = Concatenation allows SQL injection, because input can alter the statement's structure.
    #Correct. A bound parameter is always treated as a value, never as SQL. Prepared statements are also faster on repeated execution.
  ~ Concatenation does not compile in Java.
    #Incorrect. It compiles perfectly well, which is what makes the vulnerability easy to introduce.
  ~ Placeholders are needed only for numeric columns.
    #Incorrect. Every user-supplied value should be bound, and string input is the most dangerous.
  ~ Concatenation is limited to 255 characters.
    #Incorrect. No such limit exists.
}

::t15_Q6:: [Spot the bug] A DAO opens a `Connection` in every method but never closes it,
and after a while the application hangs on new queries. What is happening?
{
  = Connections are leaking until the pool or database connection limit is exhausted.
    #Correct. Use try-with-resources so Connection, Statement and ResultSet are closed on every path, including exceptions.
  ~ The database has run out of disk space.
    #Incorrect. The symptom is blocked acquisition of connections, not storage failure.
  ~ The JDBC driver caches queries and eventually stalls.
    #Incorrect. Drivers do not behave this way.
  ~ The connection string is malformed.
    #Incorrect. That would fail immediately rather than after sustained use.
}

::t15_Q7:: Which responsibility belongs in the service layer rather than the DAO?
{
  = Coordinating two DAO calls inside a single transaction to enforce a business rule.
    #Correct. The DAO handles one table's persistence. Transaction boundaries and cross-entity rules belong to the service.
  ~ Mapping a ResultSet row to a domain object.
    #Incorrect. Row mapping is core DAO work.
  ~ Building the SQL string for a query.
    #Incorrect. SQL belongs in the DAO and should not leak upwards.
  ~ Binding parameters to a PreparedStatement.
    #Incorrect. This is DAO work.
}

::t15_Q8:: Why should `findById` return `Optional<Task>` rather than `null`?
{
  = It makes possible absence part of the method signature, so the compiler pushes the caller to handle it.
    #Correct. Absence is a normal outcome of a lookup, and null carries no information about whether it was expected.
  ~ Optional is faster than returning null.
    #Incorrect. There is a small overhead; the benefit is safety and clarity.
  ~ Optional prevents the database query from failing.
    #Incorrect. It describes the result, and a genuine failure should still surface as an exception.
  ~ Because DAO methods are not permitted to return null in Java.
    #Incorrect. They may; it is simply a poor contract for a lookup.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t16_functional_interfaces

::t16_Q1:: You need to pass a behaviour to a method that will test each
element of a list and return `true` or `false` for each one. Which functional
interface from `java.util.function` is the correct choice?
{
  = `Predicate<T>`, whose single abstract method is `boolean test(T t)`.
    #Correct. `Predicate<T>` is designed for boolean-valued tests on a single input, exactly matching this requirement.
  ~ `Consumer<T>`, because it processes each element and returns a result.
    #Incorrect. `Consumer<T>` returns void; it performs a side effect rather than returning a boolean.
  ~ `Supplier<T>`, because it produces the boolean result for each element.
    #Incorrect. `Supplier<T>` takes no input; it produces a value without receiving one.
  ~ `Function<T, Boolean>`, which is equivalent and preferable because it avoids boxing.
    #Incorrect. `Function<T, Boolean>` boxes the result into `Boolean`; `Predicate<T>` is preferred for boolean tests as it avoids boxing and makes intent clearer.
}

::t16_Q2:: A list of integers is filtered with `scores.removeIf(s -> s < 10)`.
What is the role of the lambda here, and what interface does it satisfy?
{
  = The lambda is a `Predicate<Integer>` that returns true for scores below 10; `removeIf` removes every element for which the predicate returns true.
    #Correct. `removeIf` accepts a `Predicate<? super E>` and removes elements where the predicate holds.
  ~ The lambda is a `Consumer<Integer>` that processes each score and removes it from the list.
    #Incorrect. `Consumer` returns void; it cannot signal which elements to remove. `removeIf` uses a predicate.
  ~ The lambda is a `Function<Integer, Integer>` that transforms scores before filtering.
    #Incorrect. `removeIf` does not transform values; it removes elements based on a boolean test.
  ~ The lambda is a `Supplier<Boolean>` that provides the removal decision without taking input.
    #Incorrect. `Supplier` takes no input; the predicate must receive each score to evaluate it.
}

::t16_Q3:: Two `Predicate<String>` objects, `longEnough` and `hasDigit`, are
combined with `longEnough.and(hasDigit)`. What does the composed predicate
return for a string that satisfies `longEnough` but not `hasDigit`?
{
  = `false`, because `and` requires both predicates to return true.
    #Correct. `Predicate.and` is a logical AND\: the composed predicate returns true only when both constituent predicates return true.
  ~ `true`, because the string passes the first predicate.
    #Incorrect. `and` requires both predicates to pass; failing either one means the composed predicate returns false.
  ~ A NullPointerException, because the second predicate is only evaluated when the first returns false.
    #Incorrect. `and` uses short-circuit evaluation\: if the first predicate returns false, the second is not evaluated. No exception occurs.
  ~ An IllegalStateException, because predicates cannot be combined with `and` at runtime.
    #Incorrect. `Predicate.and` is a standard default method available on all `Predicate` instances.
}

::t16_Q4:: A method reference `Integer\:\:parseInt` is assigned to a variable.
Which functional interface type is compatible with this assignment?
{
  = `Function<String, Integer>`, because `parseInt` takes one `String` input and returns an `Integer`.
    #Correct. The method reference matches the `apply(T t)` signature of `Function<String, Integer>`.
  ~ `Consumer<String>`, because `parseInt` consumes a String and produces no output.
    #Incorrect. `parseInt` returns an `Integer`; `Consumer` is for operations that return void.
  ~ `Supplier<Integer>`, because `parseInt` supplies an integer value.
    #Incorrect. `Supplier` takes no input; `parseInt` requires a String argument.
  ~ `BiFunction<String, Integer, Integer>`, because parsing involves two integers.
    #Incorrect. `Integer.parseInt(String)` takes exactly one argument; `BiFunction` requires two.
}


::t16_Q5:: Which functional interface matches a lambda that takes one value and returns a boolean?
{
  = Predicate<T>
    #Correct. Its single method is `boolean test(T t)`, which is what filter, removeIf and validation rules require.
  ~ Function<T, R>
    #Incorrect. Function returns an arbitrary R. Predicate is the specialised boolean form and avoids boxing.
  ~ Consumer<T>
    #Incorrect. Consumer returns void and exists for side effects.
  ~ Supplier<T>
    #Incorrect. Supplier takes no input and produces a value.
}

::t16_Q6:: [Predict the output] A `Predicate<String> notBlank \= s -> !s.isBlank();`
is combined as `notBlank.and(s -> s.length() > 3)`. What does the combined predicate
return for the input "abc"?
{
  = false, because the second condition fails.
    #Correct. `and` requires both to hold. "abc" is not blank but its length is 3, which is not greater than 3.
  ~ true, because the first condition passes.
    #Incorrect. `and` is a conjunction, so both must pass.
  ~ true, because `and` returns the first result.
    #Incorrect. That would be the behaviour of neither `and` nor `or`.
  ~ The code throws NullPointerException.
    #Incorrect. The input is a valid non-null string.
}

::t16_Q7:: Which method reference is equivalent to the lambda `s -> s.toUpperCase()`?
{
  = String::toUpperCase
    #Correct. This is an unbound instance method reference: the single argument becomes the receiver.
  ~ String::new
    #Incorrect. That is a constructor reference, matching a Supplier or Function that builds a String.
  ~ s::toUpperCase
    #Incorrect. That binds a specific existing instance and takes no argument.
  ~ String.toUpperCase()
    #Incorrect. That is a call, not a reference, and toUpperCase is not static.
}

::t16_Q8:: [Spot the bug] A stream uses `.filter(t -> \{ count++; return t.isDone(); \})`
to count matches while filtering. Why is this a poor idea?
{
  = A predicate must be side-effect-free, since streams may skip, reorder or parallelise calls.
    #Correct. Short-circuiting means the predicate may not run for every element at all. Use `.filter(Task::isDone).count()` instead.
  ~ Lambdas cannot contain a statement block.
    #Incorrect. Block-bodied lambdas with an explicit return are valid.
  ~ `count++` will not compile inside a lambda.
    #Incorrect. It will not compile on a local variable, which must be effectively final, but does on a field. The design problem remains either way.
  ~ filter cannot accept a Predicate.
    #Incorrect. Predicate is exactly what filter takes.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t17_streams_api

::t17_Q1:: [Predict the output] A pipeline is `list.stream().filter(...).map(...)` with no
terminal operation. What happens when it runs?
{
  = Nothing. Intermediate operations are lazy and do no work until a terminal operation is invoked.
    #Correct. This is why a lone `peek` prints nothing and is a common debugging trap.
  ~ The filter and map both run over every element.
    #Incorrect. Without a terminal operation the pipeline is only described, never executed.
  ~ The code throws IllegalStateException.
    #Incorrect. It compiles and runs; it simply does nothing observable.
  ~ The original list is modified in place.
    #Incorrect. Streams never modify their source.
}

::t17_Q2:: A stream is `filter`, then `map`, then `findFirst`, and the first element
passes the filter. How many elements does `map` process?
{
  = One, because findFirst short-circuits as soon as it has a result.
    #Correct. Elements are pulled through the whole pipeline one at a time, not stage by stage over the entire collection.
  ~ All of them, since map runs before findFirst.
    #Incorrect. That would be true of eager stage-by-stage evaluation, which is not how streams work.
  ~ None, because findFirst reads from the source directly.
    #Incorrect. The element must pass through map to be produced.
  ~ Two, because findFirst needs to compare candidates.
    #Incorrect. It returns the first available element without comparison.
}

::t17_Q3:: When must you use `flatMap` rather than `map`?
{
  = When each element yields multiple values that should be flattened into one stream.
    #Correct. The tell is a nested result type such as Stream<List<T>>, which means map was used where flatMap was needed.
  ~ When the transformation may return null.
    #Incorrect. Null handling is a separate concern and is better addressed with a filter.
  ~ When the stream is parallel.
    #Incorrect. Both work in parallel streams.
  ~ When the result type differs from the input type.
    #Incorrect. That is ordinary map.
}

::t17_Q4:: Why does re-using a stream throw IllegalStateException?
{
  = A stream is a one-pass view over its source, and the terminal operation consumes it.
    #Correct. Re-create it from the collection instead; `list.stream()` is cheap and should never be cached in a field.
  ~ Streams are immutable and cannot be assigned twice.
    #Incorrect. The restriction concerns consumption, not assignment.
  ~ The garbage collector reclaims the stream immediately.
    #Incorrect. Collection timing is unrelated.
  ~ Only parallel streams may be reused.
    #Incorrect. The restriction applies equally to both.
}

::t17_Q5:: Which collector produces a count per group rather than a list per group?
{
  = `Collectors.groupingBy(Task::getCategory, Collectors.counting())`
    #Correct. The downstream collector replaces the default toList(). Note that counting() yields Long.
  ~ `Collectors.groupingBy(Task::getCategory)`
    #Incorrect. The one-argument form defaults to toList(), giving Map<K, List<Task>>.
  ~ `Collectors.toMap(Task::getCategory, Task::getId)`
    #Incorrect. This maps each category to a single id and throws on duplicate keys.
  ~ `Collectors.partitioningBy(Task::getCategory)`
    #Incorrect. partitioningBy takes a Predicate and always produces exactly two groups.
}

::t17_Q6:: [Spot the bug] `stream.forEach(t -> results.add(transform(t)))` on a parallel
stream produces a list with missing or duplicated entries. Why?
{
  = An ordinary ArrayList is not thread-safe, and forEach with a side effect writes to it concurrently.
    #Correct. Use `.map(this::transform).collect(Collectors.toList())`, which handles the combination safely.
  ~ forEach cannot be used on a parallel stream.
    #Incorrect. It can, but its side effects are the caller's responsibility.
  ~ transform must be declared static.
    #Incorrect. Unrelated to the failure.
  ~ Parallel streams silently drop elements.
    #Incorrect. They do not; the corruption comes from unsynchronised mutation.
}

::t17_Q7:: When is `reduce` the right choice over `sum()` or `count()`?
{
  = When the combining operation is custom, such as multiplying values or folding into an accumulator.
    #Correct. The operation must be associative and side-effect-free, or parallel execution gives wrong results.
  ~ Whenever the stream contains numbers.
    #Incorrect. For plain totals, `mapToInt(...).sum()` is clearer and avoids boxing.
  ~ Whenever the stream might be empty.
    #Incorrect. Both handle empty streams; reduce without an identity returns an Optional.
  ~ Only on parallel streams.
    #Incorrect. It works on sequential streams too.
}

::t17_Q8:: What advantage does `IntStream` have over `Stream<Integer>`?
{
  = It avoids boxing each value into an Integer object, and offers sum, average and max directly.
    #Correct. Use mapToInt to switch, which matters most on large numeric datasets.
  ~ It can be reused after a terminal operation.
    #Incorrect. All streams are single-use.
  ~ It is always executed in parallel.
    #Incorrect. Parallelism is opt-in via parallel().
  ~ It permits null elements.
    #Incorrect. A primitive int cannot be null.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t18_io

::t18_Q1:: Why is `Files.lines()` preferred over `Files.readAllLines()` for a very large file?
{
  = It streams lazily, so memory use stays roughly constant instead of holding every line at once.
    #Correct. readAllLines on a multi-gigabyte file risks OutOfMemoryError even if you need only a few lines.
  ~ It reads the file more quickly in all cases.
    #Incorrect. For small files the difference is negligible; the benefit is memory and short-circuiting.
  ~ It does not require the file to exist.
    #Incorrect. Both throw if the path cannot be read.
  ~ It automatically closes the file without try-with-resources.
    #Incorrect. It specifically must be closed, because it holds an open file handle.
}

::t18_Q2:: [Spot the bug] `Files.lines(path)` is used without try-with-resources in a
long-running program, which eventually fails with "Too many open files". Why?
{
  = The stream holds an open file handle that is never released, so descriptors accumulate.
    #Correct. Unlike a stream over a List, this one is backed by a real file and is AutoCloseable.
  ~ Files.lines opens the file twice.
    #Incorrect. It opens it once; the problem is that it is never closed.
  ~ The operating system limit applies only to writes.
    #Incorrect. Open descriptors count regardless of direction.
  ~ The file must be deleted after reading.
    #Incorrect. Deleting is unrelated to releasing the handle.
}

::t18_Q3:: Why must `IOException` be caught or declared when calling `Files.readAllLines()`?
{
  = It is a checked exception, because file operations fail for reasons outside the program's control.
    #Correct. Permissions, deletion between check and read, a full disk or a dropped network share are all possible.
  ~ Because the method is static.
    #Incorrect. Static has no bearing on exception checking.
  ~ Because the file might contain invalid characters.
    #Incorrect. That would surface as a decoding error, and is not why the exception is checked.
  ~ Because all Java methods that touch disk return an exception object.
    #Incorrect. They throw rather than return, and not all are checked.
}

::t18_Q4:: What is the most efficient way to read only the first ten lines of a huge file?
{
  = `Files.lines(path)` with `.limit(10)`, inside try-with-resources.
    #Correct. Laziness means the file is read only far enough to produce ten lines.
  ~ `Files.readAllLines(path).subList(0, 10)`
    #Incorrect. This reads the entire file into memory first, which is exactly what should be avoided.
  ~ Read the whole file into a String and split on newline.
    #Incorrect. Worse still: it loads everything and then allocates an array of every line.
  ~ Open the file ten times, reading one line each.
    #Incorrect. This re-reads from the start each time and is far slower.
}

::t18_Q5:: Which StandardOpenOption writes a file only if it does not already exist?
{
  = CREATE_NEW
    #Correct. It throws FileAlreadyExistsException if the file is present, and the check is atomic.
  ~ CREATE
    #Incorrect. CREATE makes the file if absent but happily opens an existing one.
  ~ TRUNCATE_EXISTING
    #Incorrect. This clears an existing file, which is the opposite of the requirement.
  ~ APPEND
    #Incorrect. This adds to the end of an existing file.
}

::t18_Q6:: Why is `CREATE_NEW` better than testing `Files.exists(path)` and then writing?
{
  = The check-then-write sequence is a race condition; CREATE_NEW performs the test and creation atomically.
    #Correct. Between the check and the write, another process or thread could create the file and have it overwritten.
  ~ Files.exists is deprecated.
    #Incorrect. It is current and useful in other contexts.
  ~ Files.exists cannot be used with a Path.
    #Incorrect. Path is exactly what it takes.
  ~ CREATE_NEW is faster to execute.
    #Incorrect. Correctness under concurrency, not speed, is the reason.
}

::t18_Q7:: What advantage does `BufferedReader` have over reading a file one character at a time?
{
  = It reads a block into memory at once, greatly reducing the number of system calls.
    #Correct. Unbuffered character-by-character reading makes an expensive call per character.
  ~ It can read files that BufferedReader-less code cannot open.
    #Incorrect. Both can open the same files.
  ~ It automatically parses CSV fields.
    #Incorrect. Splitting lines into fields remains your responsibility.
  ~ It removes the need to handle IOException.
    #Incorrect. Its methods still throw IOException.
}

::t18_Q8:: [Predict the output] A CSV line `"Ann,,25"` is processed with `line.split(",")`.
What is the resulting array length?
{
  = 3, with an empty string in the middle position.
    #Correct. split preserves empty fields in the middle. Note that trailing empty fields ARE dropped unless a negative limit is passed.
  ~ 2, because the empty field is skipped.
    #Incorrect. Only trailing empty strings are removed by default.
  ~ 4, because the double comma creates an extra element.
    #Incorrect. Two commas delimit three fields.
  ~ 1, because split requires a regular expression.
    #Incorrect. A comma is a valid regex matching itself.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t19_concurrency

::t19_Q1:: A developer writes `thread.run()` instead of `thread.start()`.
What is the consequence?
{
  = The `run()` method executes on the current (calling) thread — no new thread is created.
    #Correct. `run()` is just a regular method call; only `start()` asks the JVM to schedule a new thread and eventually call `run()` on it.
  ~ A new thread is created but it terminates immediately without executing `run`.
    #Incorrect. `run()` called directly executes synchronously on the current thread; no new thread is involved at all.
  ~ A compile error occurs because `run()` is not a public method on `Thread`.
    #Incorrect. `run()` is public; the code compiles and runs without error — it just does not create a new thread.
  ~ The thread enters a BLOCKED state waiting for the JVM scheduler to call `run()`.
    #Incorrect. Calling `run()` directly bypasses the scheduler entirely and executes immediately on the caller's thread.
}

::t19_Q2:: A `Counter` class has `private int _count` and a non-synchronised
`increment()` method that performs `_count++`. One thousand threads each call
`increment()` once. Why is the final value of `_count` likely to be less than
1000?
{
  = `_count++` is not atomic: it involves a read, an increment, and a write. Two threads can read the same value before either writes back, losing an increment.
    #Correct. This is a classic race condition — without synchronisation, concurrent read-modify-write sequences can overlap and produce an incorrect final count.
  ~ Threads automatically queue their increments so the count is always exactly 1000.
    #Incorrect. Without synchronisation, the JVM provides no automatic queuing of concurrent writes to shared state.
  ~ The JVM serialises all primitive operations, so `_count++` is inherently thread-safe.
    #Incorrect. The JVM does not serialise compound operations on primitives; `_count++` is three separate operations that can interleave.
  ~ Integer overflow causes the count to wrap around to a value below 1000.
    #Incorrect. With only 1000 increments, integer overflow is not a factor; the issue is a race condition.
}

::t19_Q3:: `Executors.newCachedThreadPool()` is recommended for a server that handles client connections. Why is it preferred over `newFixedThreadPool(1)` for this use case?
{
  = A cached pool grows as concurrent connections increase and reuses idle threads when connections decrease, handling variable load without queuing clients behind each other.
    #Correct. A single-thread fixed pool would serialise all client handling, causing each new client to wait for the previous one to finish.
  ~ A cached pool is synchronised by default, preventing race conditions in client handlers.
    #Incorrect. Thread pool type does not determine whether shared state in handlers is synchronised.
  ~ A cached pool automatically balances load across multiple machines.
    #Incorrect. `ExecutorService` operates within a single JVM; distributed load balancing is a separate concern.
  ~ A cached pool limits the total number of threads to the number of available CPU cores.
    #Incorrect. A cached pool creates as many threads as needed; `newWorkStealingPool` relates to CPU cores.
}

::t19_Q4:: After submitting tasks to an `ExecutorService`, the developer omits
`pool.shutdown()`. What is the most likely observable problem?
{
  = The JVM does not exit after `main()` returns because the non-daemon pool threads are still running and keeping the process alive.
    #Correct. `ExecutorService` threads are non-daemon by default; the JVM will not exit while they are alive.
  ~ All submitted tasks are immediately cancelled and their results are lost.
    #Incorrect. Tasks that have already started or been queued will continue running; the issue is that the JVM stays alive.
  ~ A NullPointerException is thrown when the pool attempts to clean up its threads.
    #Incorrect. Without a shutdown call, the pool simply keeps its threads idle and running; no exception is thrown.
  ~ The pool automatically shuts down after 60 seconds of inactivity.
    #Incorrect. `ExecutorService` does not have a built-in idle timeout for shutdown; you must call `shutdown()` explicitly.
}


::t19_Q5:: What is the difference between `execute()` and `submit()` on an ExecutorService?
{
  = `submit` returns a Future and captures any thrown exception inside it, whereas `execute` returns void and lets the exception reach the thread's handler.
    #Correct. A task submitted with `submit` can fail silently if nobody calls `Future.get()`.
  ~ `execute` runs the task on the calling thread; `submit` uses the pool.
    #Incorrect. Both hand the task to the pool.
  ~ `submit` runs tasks sequentially; `execute` runs them in parallel.
    #Incorrect. The pool governs concurrency, not the choice of method.
  ~ They are identical aliases.
    #Incorrect. The return type and the error-handling behaviour differ significantly.
}

::t19_Q6:: [Spot the bug] Two threads each increment a shared `int counter` one
thousand times, and the final total is less than 2000. Why?
{
  = `counter++` is a read-modify-write sequence, so two threads can read the same value and one update is lost.
    #Correct. Use AtomicInteger, or synchronise the increment. The failure is timing-dependent, so it often passes on a single run.
  ~ The JVM caps integer increments across threads.
    #Incorrect. No such cap exists.
  ~ One thread finished before the other started.
    #Incorrect. That would still yield 2000.
  ~ `int` overflows at 2000.
    #Incorrect. `int` holds values above two billion.
}

::t19_Q7:: What happens if a server never calls `pool.shutdown()` before `main` returns?
{
  = The JVM does not exit, because the pool's threads are non-daemon and keep the process alive.
    #Correct. The program appears to hang after finishing its work. Call shutdown, then awaitTermination.
  ~ The pool's tasks are cancelled and the JVM exits immediately.
    #Incorrect. Non-daemon threads prevent JVM termination.
  ~ The program exits but leaks memory to the operating system.
    #Incorrect. The OS reclaims everything once a process actually exits; the problem is that it does not.
  ~ A CompletionException is thrown from main.
    #Incorrect. No exception occurs; the process simply never terminates.
}

::t19_Q8:: Why is calling `myRunnable.run()` different from `new Thread(myRunnable).start()`?
{
  = `run()` executes the body on the current thread; only `start()` asks the JVM for a new thread.
    #Correct. Printing `Thread.currentThread().getName()` confirms it: `run()` reports main for every task.
  ~ `run()` is faster but otherwise identical.
    #Incorrect. No new thread is created at all, so there is no concurrency.
  ~ `run()` creates a thread but does not start it.
    #Incorrect. It creates no thread; it is an ordinary method call.
  ~ `start()` may be called repeatedly on one Thread object, unlike run().
    #Incorrect. The reverse is true: a second `start()` throws IllegalThreadStateException.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t20_json_1_jackson_basics

::t20_Q1:: JSON has exactly six value types. Which of the following is NOT a
valid JSON value type?
{
  = Date, used to represent calendar values such as `2026-04-14`.
    #Correct (it is the NOT answer). JSON has no date type; dates must be encoded as strings or numbers.
  ~ String, surrounded by double quotes such as `"hello"`.
    #Incorrect — String IS one of the six valid JSON types.
  ~ Array, a comma-separated list of values in square brackets such as `[1, 2, 3]`.
    #Incorrect — Array IS one of the six valid JSON types.
  ~ Boolean, either `true` or `false` in lowercase.
    #Incorrect — Boolean IS one of the six valid JSON types.
}

::t20_Q2:: A developer writes `mapper.readValue(json, List<Player>.class)` but
it does not compile. What is the root cause and how is it corrected?
{
  = Java's type erasure means generic type parameters are not available at runtime, so `List<Player>.class` is invalid syntax; the fix is to use `new TypeReference<List<Player>>()\{\}`.
    #Correct. `TypeReference` captures the full generic type as an anonymous subclass, preserving it through erasure so Jackson can deserialise correctly.
  ~ `List<Player>.class` fails because `List` is an interface and interfaces do not have a `.class` literal.
    #Incorrect. While `List` is an interface, `List.class` is valid; the problem is the generic parameter, not the interface status.
  ~ The `ObjectMapper` cannot deserialise into a `List` type; an array `Player[]` must be used instead.
    #Incorrect. Jackson can deserialise into `List<Player>` when given a `TypeReference`; both array and list forms work.
  ~ `readValue` does not accept a class literal as the second argument; a `JsonParser` must be used.
    #Incorrect. `readValue(String, Class<T>)` is a valid overload; the issue is specifically with parameterised generic types.
}

::t20_Q3:: A JSON object key uses the name `"player_score"` but the
corresponding Java field is named `playerScore`. Deserialisation produces a
null value for the field. What annotation resolves this mismatch?
{
  = `@JsonProperty("player_score")` placed on the field or its getter, mapping the JSON key to the Java field name.
    #Correct. `@JsonProperty` allows you to specify the exact JSON key name that Jackson should read or write for a given field.
  ~ `@JsonIgnore("player_score")` to tell Jackson to skip the mismatched field.
    #Incorrect. `@JsonIgnore` excludes a field from serialisation/deserialisation entirely; it does not rename it.
  ~ Rename the Java field to `player_score` because Jackson requires exact name matching.
    #Incorrect. While renaming is one option, `@JsonProperty` is the designed solution for mapping different names without changing field names.
  ~ `@JsonIgnoreProperties(\{"player_score"\})` to suppress the unknown key warning.
    #Incorrect. `@JsonIgnoreProperties` silently ignores unknown JSON keys; it does not bind them to a differently named field.
}

::t20_Q4:: When using JSON as a wire protocol over a TCP socket, which
`ObjectMapper` method pair is used to convert objects to strings and back,
and why are file-based overloads not used?
{
  = `writeValueAsString` to serialise and `readValue(String, ...)` to deserialise, because socket communication operates on in-memory strings rather than disk files.
    #Correct. File-based overloads write to or read from disk; socket-based communication keeps everything in memory as strings sent via PrintWriter/BufferedReader.
  ~ `writeValue(File, ...)` and `readValue(File, ...)`, because TCP sockets are treated as virtual files by the operating system.
    #Incorrect. While sockets are file descriptors at the OS level, the Jackson API distinction is between `File`/`Path` objects and in-memory strings; you use string methods for socket communication.
  ~ `writeBytes` and `readBytes`, because TCP transmits raw byte arrays not character strings.
    #Incorrect. While TCP is byte-based, the idiomatic Java socket pattern wraps the byte streams in `PrintWriter`/`BufferedReader` for line-based text communication; Jackson's `writeValueAsString` produces the text.
  ~ `prettyPrint(Object)` and `parse(String)`, which are convenience aliases added in Java 17.
    #Incorrect. These method names do not exist on `ObjectMapper`; this option is fictional.
}

::t20_Q5:: What does Jackson require of a class in order to deserialise JSON into it?
{
  = A no-argument constructor plus setters, or a constructor annotated so Jackson can bind the properties.
    #Correct. Without a way to construct and populate the object, readValue throws InvalidDefinitionException.
  ~ Only that the class implements Serializable.
    #Incorrect. Java serialisation is unrelated to Jackson's JSON binding.
  ~ Only that every field is public.
    #Incorrect. Jackson uses getters and setters by default rather than direct field access.
  ~ That the class extends ObjectMapper.
    #Incorrect. ObjectMapper is the tool performing the conversion, not a base class.
}

::t20_Q6:: How does Jackson derive the JSON key from a getter named `getTrackId()`?
{
  = It strips the `get` prefix and lower-cases the first letter, producing `trackId`.
    #Correct. The field name is irrelevant, which is why a field named `_trackId` still serialises as `trackId`.
  ~ It uses the private field name exactly as declared.
    #Incorrect. By default Jackson works from the accessor, not the field.
  ~ It uses the full method name, `getTrackId`.
    #Incorrect. The prefix is removed as part of the JavaBean naming convention.
  ~ It converts the name to snake_case as `track_id`.
    #Incorrect. That requires an explicit naming strategy to be configured.
}

::t20_Q7:: [Spot the bug] `mapper.readValue(json, List.class)` returns a list whose
elements turn out to be LinkedHashMap rather than Player, causing a ClassCastException.
What is the fix?
{
  = Supply the full generic type with `new TypeReference<List<Player>>() \{\}`.
    #Correct. Type erasure removes the element type from `List.class`, so Jackson defaults to maps. TypeReference preserves it.
  ~ Cast the result to `List<Player>` before use.
    #Incorrect. The cast compiles with a warning but the elements are genuinely maps, so it still fails.
  ~ Change the field type to an array and cast afterwards.
    #Incorrect. `Player[].class` does work, but the underlying issue is the missing generic type information.
  ~ Add Serializable to the Player class.
    #Incorrect. Unrelated to Jackson's handling of generics.
}

::t20_Q8:: Why does a line-delimited JSON protocol require that each message contains no raw newline?
{
  = The receiver uses readLine(), so an embedded newline would split one message into two unparseable fragments.
    #Correct. `writeValueAsString` produces single-line JSON by default, which is why pretty-printing must not be enabled on the wire.
  ~ JSON does not permit newline characters anywhere.
    #Incorrect. JSON tolerates whitespace between tokens; the constraint here comes from the framing choice.
  ~ Newlines increase the message size beyond the socket buffer.
    #Incorrect. Size is not the issue; message framing is.
  ~ Jackson cannot parse a string containing a newline.
    #Incorrect. It can. The problem occurs before parsing, when the line is read.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t21_json_2_jackson_advanced

::t21_Q1:: Why should a request `type` string be looked up in a handler map rather
than used to invoke a method of that name by reflection?
{
  = The type comes from an untrusted client, and reflection would let a caller reach arbitrary methods.
    #Correct. A map is a whitelist: unknown types fall through to a clean error and nothing else is reachable.
  ~ Reflection is not available in Java.
    #Incorrect. It is available, which is what makes the approach dangerous rather than impossible.
  ~ Method names cannot contain underscores.
    #Incorrect. They can; naming is not the issue.
  ~ A map lookup is the only way to compare strings.
    #Incorrect. Strings can be compared directly; the reason is security and decoupling.
}

::t21_Q2:: [Spot the bug] `payload.get("id").asInt()` is called on a request whose
payload has no "id" field. What happens?
{
  = `get` returns null and the call throws NullPointerException on the server thread.
    #Correct. Check `has("id")` first, or use a safe accessor, and reply with a structured error instead of crashing the handler.
  ~ It returns 0 safely.
    #Incorrect. asInt() would return 0 for a present but non-numeric node; a missing node yields null first.
  ~ Jackson creates the field automatically with a default value.
    #Incorrect. Jackson never invents fields.
  ~ It throws JsonProcessingException, which is checked.
    #Incorrect. The failure is a NullPointerException at the call site.
}

::t21_Q3:: A payload contains `\{"id": "abc"\}` and the server calls `asInt()` on it.
What is the result, and why is that dangerous?
{
  = It returns 0 silently, so the server proceeds with wrong data and nothing reports an error.
    #Correct. Jackson's asInt is lenient. Wrong data is worse than a crash because it is invisible.
  ~ It throws NumberFormatException immediately.
    #Incorrect. asInt does not throw for a non-numeric node; it falls back to a default.
  ~ It returns -1 to indicate failure.
    #Incorrect. The default is 0, not a sentinel.
  ~ It converts "abc" using its character codes.
    #Incorrect. No such conversion occurs.
}

::t21_Q4:: Why must binary data be Base64-encoded before being placed in a JSON string?
{
  = JSON strings are text, and arbitrary bytes are not valid UTF-8 and could terminate or corrupt the string.
    #Correct. Base64 maps any byte sequence onto 64 safe ASCII characters, at roughly a 33 per cent size increase.
  ~ Because JSON has no string type.
    #Incorrect. Strings are a core JSON type.
  ~ Because Jackson cannot serialise byte arrays at all.
    #Incorrect. It handles byte[] by Base64-encoding it, which is precisely the mechanism in question.
  ~ Because Base64 compresses the data.
    #Incorrect. It expands the data by about a third.
}

::t21_Q5:: When would Base64-in-JSON be the wrong choice?
{
  = For large files, because the payload grows by a third and must be held in memory as a String on both sides.
    #Correct. Prefer a separate binary channel or multipart upload, keeping JSON for the metadata.
  ~ For any file containing text.
    #Incorrect. Text files encode perfectly well, though encoding them is often unnecessary.
  ~ Whenever the transport is a socket.
    #Incorrect. Sockets are exactly where line-delimited JSON with Base64 is convenient for modest payloads.
  ~ When the receiving language is not Java.
    #Incorrect. Base64 is a language-independent standard.
}

::t21_Q6:: Why should a metadata-only query list its columns rather than use `SELECT *`?
{
  = `SELECT *` fetches the BLOB column, transferring large binary data the caller never uses.
    #Correct. Listing 200 rows would pull 200 images across the network to display 200 titles.
  ~ `SELECT *` is invalid SQL in MySQL.
    #Incorrect. It is valid, just wasteful and fragile here.
  ~ Column names are required by PreparedStatement.
    #Incorrect. PreparedStatement accepts any valid SQL.
  ~ `SELECT *` cannot be used with a WHERE clause.
    #Incorrect. It can.
}

::t21_Q7:: Which JDBC methods store and retrieve a byte array in a BLOB column?
{
  = `setBytes` on the PreparedStatement and `getBytes` on the ResultSet.
    #Correct. For very large objects the streaming variants setBinaryStream and getBinaryStream avoid loading everything into memory.
  ~ `setString` and `getString`.
    #Incorrect. These are for character data and would corrupt arbitrary bytes.
  ~ `setObject` and `getObject` only.
    #Incorrect. These work in some drivers but are untyped and less clear than the byte-specific methods.
  ~ `setBlob` is required; there is no byte array option.
    #Incorrect. setBytes is the common and simpler approach for moderate sizes.
}

::t21_Q8:: What specifically does a JSON round-trip test verify?
{
  = That an object serialised and then deserialised equals the original, so nothing was lost or renamed in transit.
    #Correct. It catches missing getters or setters, absent no-arg constructors, key mismatches and type drift - and it requires a correct equals.
  ~ That the JSON string matches a fixed expected text exactly.
    #Incorrect. That test is brittle, since key order and formatting may vary.
  ~ That the object can be written to a file.
    #Incorrect. File IO is not involved.
  ~ That the class implements Serializable.
    #Incorrect. Java serialisation is unrelated to Jackson.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t22_networking

::t22_Q1:: In the echo server example, `new PrintWriter(clientSocket.getOutputStream(), true)`
is used. What does the second argument `true` control?
{
  = Auto-flush\: the output buffer is flushed automatically after every `println` call, ensuring data is sent to the client immediately.
    #Correct. Without auto-flush, data may remain in the PrintWriter buffer and never reach the client until the buffer fills or the stream is closed.
  ~ Thread-safety\: the PrintWriter locks the stream so multiple threads can write concurrently.
    #Incorrect. `PrintWriter` is not made thread-safe by this flag; the `true` argument controls auto-flushing.
  ~ Character encoding\: `true` instructs the writer to use UTF-8 instead of the platform default.
    #Incorrect. Character encoding is set separately via a charset parameter or `OutputStreamWriter`; `true` here means auto-flush.
  ~ Error handling\: exceptions thrown by the underlying stream are rethrown rather than suppressed.
    #Incorrect. `PrintWriter` suppresses exceptions by default; to check for errors you call `checkError()`. The `true` flag controls flush behaviour.
}

::t22_Q2:: A multi-client server uses `serverSocket.accept()` inside a
`while (true)` loop and calls `pool.submit(new ClientHandler(clientSocket))`
for each connection. Why does the server accept a second client before the
first finishes?
{
  = Each `ClientHandler` runs on a separate pool thread, so `accept()` returns to the loop immediately and can accept new connections concurrently.
    #Correct. `submit` is non-blocking\: the work is handed off to the pool and the accept loop continues without waiting.
  ~ `accept()` is non-blocking in Java and returns null if no second client is ready.
    #Incorrect. `accept()` is blocking — it blocks until a client connects. It is the `submit()` call that is non-blocking.
  ~ The second client waits in a queue inside `ServerSocket` and is accepted only after the first disconnects.
    #Incorrect. The server accepts the second connection as soon as `accept()` is called again, which happens immediately after submitting the first handler.
  ~ Java automatically forks a new process for each client connection.
    #Incorrect. Java does not fork processes for socket connections; thread pools are used within the same JVM process.
}

::t22_Q3:: The protocol requires each JSON message to be sent as a single line
terminated by a newline. Why is sending multi-line JSON over a raw socket
problematic when using `BufferedReader.readLine()` to receive it?
{
  = `readLine()` reads exactly one line and stops at the first newline, so only the first line of the multi-line JSON is received, resulting in a parse error.
    #Correct. The receiver reads one `\n`-terminated line and tries to parse it as complete JSON; a multi-line object split across lines cannot be parsed correctly.
  ~ `BufferedReader` automatically reassembles multi-line messages because TCP guarantees message boundaries.
    #Incorrect. TCP is a byte-stream protocol with no concept of message boundaries; `readLine()` splits on `\n` without knowledge of JSON structure.
  ~ Multi-line JSON is rejected by Jackson's `ObjectMapper` because it requires compact format.
    #Incorrect. Jackson can parse pretty-printed multi-line JSON; the problem is the socket framing, not Jackson's parser.
  ~ Sending multi-line JSON causes a ConcurrentModificationException on the server.
    #Incorrect. ConcurrentModificationException is a Java collections concern; it has nothing to do with JSON framing over sockets.
}

::t22_Q4:: The `ServerResponse<T>` class uses a generic type parameter. When
the server serialises a `ServerResponse<List<Task>>` to JSON with Jackson,
what must the client do to correctly deserialise the JSON back to the same type?
{
  = Use a `TypeReference<ServerResponse<List<Task>>>` in the `readValue` call to preserve the full generic type information at runtime.
    #Correct. Due to type erasure, `ServerResponse.class` loses the generic parameter; `TypeReference` captures it as an anonymous subclass, allowing Jackson to reconstruct the nested generic type.
  ~ Pass `ServerResponse.class` to `readValue` — Jackson infers the generic parameter automatically.
    #Incorrect. Type erasure means `ServerResponse.class` carries no information about `List<Task>`; Jackson cannot infer it.
  ~ Cast the deserialized `Object` to `ServerResponse<List<Task>>` after calling `readValue(json, Object.class)`.
    #Incorrect. Casting to a generic type produces an unchecked cast warning and does not restore type information; individual element types remain unresolved.
  ~ Store the tasks in a plain `List` field without generics so that deserialisation works without TypeReference.
    #Incorrect. Using a raw `List` loses type safety and is the problem that `TypeReference` was designed to solve.
}


::t22_Q5:: Why must a server hand each accepted socket to a separate thread or pool task?
{
  = Otherwise the accept loop blocks handling one client and no other client can connect.
    #Correct. Handler work is mostly blocked on socket reads, so a single-threaded server serves exactly one client at a time.
  ~ Because ServerSocket.accept() can only be called once.
    #Incorrect. It is called repeatedly in a loop, once per connection.
  ~ Because each socket requires its own port number.
    #Incorrect. All clients connect to the same listening port.
  ~ Because TCP requires one thread per connection.
    #Incorrect. TCP imposes no such requirement; this is a design choice about responsiveness.
}

::t22_Q6:: [Spot the bug] A server writes a response with `out.println(json)` but the
client blocks forever on `readLine()`. The writer was created as `new PrintWriter(socket.getOutputStream())`.
What is wrong?
{
  = Auto-flush is off, so the response is buffered and never reaches the client.
    #Correct. Pass `true` as the second argument, or call `out.flush()` after each write. Both sides then block waiting for each other.
  ~ readLine() cannot read JSON.
    #Incorrect. It reads any line of text, which is why the protocol is line-delimited.
  ~ The client must call flush() before reading.
    #Incorrect. Flushing applies to the side that is writing.
  ~ PrintWriter cannot be used with sockets.
    #Incorrect. It is a standard choice for text protocols.
}

::t22_Q7:: An exception escapes `ClientHandler.run()`. What is the consequence?
{
  = That handler thread dies, the client's connection is dropped with no error response, and the socket leaks.
    #Correct. With `submit`, the exception is also captured silently in the Future. Catch inside run, reply with an error, and close in a finally block.
  ~ The server process terminates immediately.
    #Incorrect. Only the affected thread ends; the server keeps accepting.
  ~ The exception propagates to the accept loop, which can handle it.
    #Incorrect. Exceptions do not cross thread boundaries.
  ~ The pool automatically retries the task.
    #Incorrect. ExecutorService performs no retries.
}

::t22_Q8:: You must add a new entity to an existing protocol without breaking deployed clients. What is the safe approach?
{
  = Add new request types and leave every existing type's name, payload and response shape unchanged.
    #Correct. Adding is backward-compatible; renaming, removing or retyping a field is not.
  ~ Rename existing request types to a consistent new scheme at the same time.
    #Incorrect. Every deployed client sending the old names would break immediately.
  ~ Change the response wrapper to include the new entity in every reply.
    #Incorrect. Altering an existing response shape risks breaking clients that parse it strictly.
  ~ Require all clients to upgrade before the server is deployed.
    #Incorrect. Sometimes unavoidable in practice, but it is the opposite of a backward-compatible change.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t23_unit_testing

::t23_Q1:: Why is `assertNotNull(new Player("Ann"))` considered a worthless test?
{
  = `new` can never return null, so the assertion passes even if the constructor does nothing.
    #Correct. It also inflates coverage without adding safety. Assert an observable consequence instead.
  ~ assertNotNull is deprecated in JUnit 5.
    #Incorrect. It is current and useful where a value legitimately might be null.
  ~ Constructors cannot be tested.
    #Incorrect. They should be, by checking field values and rejected arguments.
  ~ The test needs a @BeforeEach method to be valid.
    #Incorrect. Setup is unrelated to the weakness of the assertion.
}

::t23_Q2:: What do the three sections of the AAA pattern represent?
{
  = Arrange the inputs, Act by calling the one thing under test, Assert the outcome.
    #Correct. Keeping them visually separate makes a test readable and reveals when it is doing too much.
  ~ Allocate, Analyse, Archive.
    #Incorrect. Not a testing convention.
  ~ Assert, Act, Arrange - executed in that order.
    #Incorrect. The order is reversed; you cannot assert before acting.
  ~ Assemble, Annotate, Apply.
    #Incorrect. Not a testing convention.
}

::t23_Q3:: Are DAO tests that connect to MySQL unit tests or integration tests?
{
  = Integration tests, because they exercise several components including an external database.
    #Correct. The file may be named ...Test.java, but they are slower and depend on the database being available.
  ~ Unit tests, because each one tests a single DAO method.
    #Incorrect. A unit test must not depend on an external system.
  ~ Neither; they are system tests.
    #Incorrect. System tests exercise the whole application end to end.
  ~ Unit tests, provided each runs in under a second.
    #Incorrect. Speed is a symptom of the category, not its definition.
}

::t23_Q4:: A project reports 80 per cent line coverage and every test passes, yet a
bug reaches production. How is that possible?
{
  = Coverage records which lines ran, not whether their behaviour was actually checked.
    #Correct. A test that calls a method and asserts nothing still counts as covering it. Untested branches also cluster in error handling.
  ~ Coverage tools are generally inaccurate.
    #Incorrect. They measure execution faithfully; the metric simply means less than people assume.
  ~ 80 per cent is below the minimum useful threshold.
    #Incorrect. No threshold guarantees correctness.
  ~ Passing tests prove the absence of bugs, so this cannot happen.
    #Incorrect. Tests can only demonstrate the presence of the behaviours they check.
}

::t23_Q5:: [Spot the bug] DAO tests are pointed at the development database and
`@BeforeEach` truncates the table. What is the consequence?
{
  = Every test run destroys the development data, and results are unreliable in both directions.
    #Correct. Use a dedicated test database. Leftover rows also break count assertions and can mask real failures.
  ~ Nothing, provided the tests pass.
    #Incorrect. Irreversible data loss occurs on every run.
  ~ The tests run more slowly.
    #Incorrect. Speed is not the concern.
  ~ JUnit refuses to run tests against a non-test database.
    #Incorrect. JUnit has no knowledge of which database you connect to.
}

::t23_Q6:: Why does a JSON round-trip test require `equals` to be overridden on the entity?
{
  = `assertEquals` calls equals, and deserialisation produces a new object that default identity equality rejects.
    #Correct. Without a value-based equals the assertion fails even when every field was preserved perfectly.
  ~ Because Jackson calls equals during deserialisation.
    #Incorrect. Jackson does not consult equals when building the object.
  ~ Because assertEquals compares hash codes only.
    #Incorrect. It calls equals; hashCode matters for hashed collections.
  ~ It does not; assertEquals compares field by field automatically.
    #Incorrect. There is no automatic field comparison for ordinary classes.
}

::t23_Q7:: Which test name best follows the `method_scenario_expectedBehaviour` convention?
{
  = `findById_nonexistentId_returnsEmpty`
    #Correct. A failure report names the method, the situation and the expectation without opening the file.
  ~ `testFindById`
    #Incorrect. It identifies the method but neither the scenario nor the expected outcome.
  ~ `test1`
    #Incorrect. It conveys nothing at all.
  ~ `daoTest`
    #Incorrect. Too broad, and it suggests the test covers several scenarios at once.
}

::t23_Q8:: A class is difficult to test because it reads a socket, parses JSON and
calls a DAO. What does this most likely indicate?
{
  = The class has too many responsibilities and should be split so each part is testable alone.
    #Correct. Testability is a design signal. Extracting the routing logic makes both classes straightforward to test.
  ~ That integration tests are the only option for any networked code.
    #Incorrect. Extracting the pure logic allows fast unit tests of the bulk of the behaviour.
  ~ That the class needs more assertions per test.
    #Incorrect. More assertions in one test makes failures harder to diagnose.
  ~ That JUnit cannot test classes using sockets.
    #Incorrect. It can, but such tests are slow and fragile compared with testing extracted logic.
}

$CATEGORY: $module$/t25_documenting_a_project

::t24_Q1:: Which Javadoc tag documents what a method gives back to its caller?
{
  = @return
    #Correct. It is omitted for void methods and should describe meaning, not merely restate the type.
  ~ @result
    #Incorrect. Not a Javadoc tag.
  ~ @output
    #Incorrect. Not a Javadoc tag.
  ~ @returns
    #Incorrect. The tag is singular: @return.
}

::t24_Q2:: Which tag documents an exception a method may throw, and what should it explain?
{
  = @throws, stating the condition under which the exception occurs.
    #Correct. "@throws IllegalArgumentException if name is null or blank" tells a caller what to avoid.
  ~ @exception is required for checked exceptions and @throws for unchecked ones.
    #Incorrect. They are synonyms; @throws is the conventional modern choice for both.
  ~ @error, naming the exception class.
    #Incorrect. Not a Javadoc tag.
  ~ @catch, describing how the caller should handle it.
    #Incorrect. Not a Javadoc tag.
}

::t24_Q3:: Which of these methods least needs a Javadoc comment?
{
  = A simple getter such as `getName()` that returns a field unchanged.
    #Correct. A comment saying "returns the name" adds nothing. Document contracts, constraints and surprises.
  ~ A DAO method that throws a checked exception on failure.
    #Incorrect. Callers need to know the failure conditions.
  ~ A constructor with guard clauses that reject invalid input.
    #Incorrect. The rejection rules are exactly what a caller must know.
  ~ A method whose return value may be null or empty.
    #Incorrect. That is a contract detail worth stating explicitly.
}

::t24_Q4:: Which Mermaid diagram type best shows tables, their columns and their
foreign-key relationships?
{
  = erDiagram
    #Correct. It expresses entities, attributes and cardinality, matching a relational schema directly.
  ~ sequenceDiagram
    #Incorrect. That shows messages exchanged between participants over time.
  ~ flowchart
    #Incorrect. That shows decision and process flow.
  ~ classDiagram
    #Incorrect. That shows classes and their relationships, which is a different level of abstraction.
}

::t24_Q5:: Which diagram type best documents a full request cycle from client to
server to DAO to database and back?
{
  = sequenceDiagram
    #Correct. It shows the participants, the order of messages and the return path, which is exactly a request cycle.
  ~ erDiagram
    #Incorrect. That describes the data model, not the interaction over time.
  ~ mindmap
    #Incorrect. That organises topics hierarchically.
  ~ pie
    #Incorrect. That shows proportions of a whole.
}

::t24_Q6:: What is the practical advantage of Mermaid diagrams over embedded images
in a project repository?
{
  = They are plain text, so they can be diffed, reviewed and edited alongside the code they describe.
    #Correct. An image drifts out of date silently and cannot be updated without the original tool.
  ~ They render faster than images in every viewer.
    #Incorrect. Rendering speed is not the point, and depends on the viewer.
  ~ They can display photographs.
    #Incorrect. Mermaid draws structured diagrams, not raster images.
  ~ They require no syntax to be learned.
    #Incorrect. Each diagram type has its own syntax.
}

::t24_Q7:: [Spot the bug] A class Javadoc reads "This class is a class that handles
handling of tasks." What is wrong with it?
{
  = It restates the class name without explaining responsibility, collaborators or constraints.
    #Correct. Useful class documentation says what the class is responsible for and what a reader must know to use it.
  ~ Class-level Javadoc is not permitted in Java.
    #Incorrect. It is standard and appears immediately above the class declaration.
  ~ It should be written as a line comment instead.
    #Incorrect. Javadoc must use the /** */ form to be extracted by the tool.
  ~ It is missing an @return tag.
    #Incorrect. @return applies to methods, not classes.
}

::t24_Q8:: Where should an architecture diagram showing the tiers of a project normally live?
{
  = In the repository README, so it is the first thing a reader encounters.
    #Correct. Detailed per-area diagrams can sit in the relevant module or package documentation.
  ~ Inside a Javadoc comment on the main class.
    #Incorrect. Javadoc documents an API; a whole-system view belongs at the project level.
  ~ In a separate document not stored in the repository.
    #Incorrect. Documentation kept outside version control drifts out of date quickly.
  ~ In the commit message of the final commit.
    #Incorrect. Commit messages explain a change, and are not a place readers look for architecture.
}

